var e={"tech:deepnets":{intro:`Deep learning stacks many layers of artificial neurons, each layer building more abstract features from the one below: edges, then textures, then eyes and wheels. Trained on graphics cards, which do thousands of multiplications at once, such networks learn to recognise objects from raw pixels instead of relying on features hand-designed by experts.`,how:`An image goes in as a grid of numbers; each layer multiplies them by learned weights and passes the result through a simple non-linear function. Backpropagation compares the output with the right label and nudges every weight to shrink the error. A gaming GPU runs these huge matrix multiplications tens of times faster than a CPU, turning months of training into days.`,history:`In 2006 Geoffrey Hinton and colleagues in Toronto showed that deep networks could be trained one layer at a time, reviving interest in “deep” learning. In 2009 Rajat Raina, Anand Madhavan and Andrew Ng at Stanford showed that GPUs could train such networks up to 70 times faster than CPUs. In 2012 Alex Krizhevsky, Ilya Sutskever and Hinton entered AlexNet in the ImageNet challenge and won with a top-5 error of 15.3%, against 26.2% for the runner-up. In 2013 Google bought their three-person company, DNNresearch. By 2014 nearly every leading ImageNet entry was a deep convolutional network trained on GPUs.`,facts:[`AlexNet had about 60 million parameters and 650,000 neurons (2012).`,`It trained for five to six days on two NVIDIA GTX 580 cards with 3 GB each (2012).`,`Its error was nearly 11 points lower than the runner-up's (2012).`]},"tech:tricks":{intro:`Deep networks were powerful but temperamental: they overfitted, stalled or blew up during training. A handful of simple tricks fixed most of this. Dropout switches off random neurons so none can lean on another, adaptive optimisers give each weight its own step size, and normalisation keeps the numbers flowing through each layer at a steady scale.`,how:`Dropout zeroes perhaps half the neurons on each training example, so the network behaves like a crowd of smaller networks voting. The Adam optimiser keeps running averages of each weight's gradient and its size, taking bold steps where the signal is steady and cautious ones where it is noisy. Batch normalisation rescales each layer's outputs across a batch to a fixed mean and spread, so deeper layers see stable inputs.`,history:`In 2010 Vinod Nair and Geoffrey Hinton popularised the rectified linear unit, a neuron that outputs zero for negative inputs and trains far faster than older S-shaped neurons. In 2012 Hinton's group described dropout, one of the keys to AlexNet's ImageNet win that year. In December 2014 Diederik Kingma and Jimmy Ba published Adam. In February 2015 Sergey Ioffe and Christian Szegedy at Google introduced batch normalisation, matching a top ImageNet model's accuracy with 14 times fewer training steps. In 2016 Jimmy Ba, Jamie Kiros and Hinton adapted the idea as layer normalisation, which suited language models better. By 2017 Adam was the default optimiser in most deep learning papers.`,facts:[`AlexNet used dropout at a rate of 0.5 in its two big fully connected layers (2012).`,`Batch normalisation reached the same accuracy in 14 times fewer steps (2015).`]},"tech:attention":{intro:`Attention lets a neural network decide, at each step, which parts of its input matter most. Early translation networks squeezed a whole sentence into one fixed list of numbers before writing the translation, and long sentences came out garbled. With attention, the decoder looks back over every word of the source sentence and focuses on the relevant ones as it writes.`,how:`For each word it is about to produce, the network scores every source word for relevance, turns the scores into weights that add up to one, and takes a weighted average of the source words' representations. The weights are learned, not programmed, and form a soft alignment: when writing the English “cat” the model attends mostly to the French “chat”. Because every step is differentiable, it trains with ordinary backpropagation.`,history:`In September 2014 Ilya Sutskever, Oriol Vinyals and Quoc Le at Google showed that two LSTM networks, an encoder and a decoder, could translate English into French, though each sentence had to pass through a single vector. In September 2014 Dzmitry Bahdanau, Kyunghyun Cho and Yoshua Bengio in Montreal added attention, and their model held up on long sentences where the older design collapsed. In 2015 Kelvin Xu and colleagues used attention to caption photographs, showing which part of the picture the network looked at for each word. In 2015 Minh-Thang Luong at Stanford published simpler “global” and “local” forms of attention. In September 2016 Google Translate switched Chinese to English to a neural system with attention, GNMT, which cut translation errors by about 60% in human tests.`,facts:[`Google's neural translator stacked 8 encoder and 8 decoder LSTM layers with attention (2016).`,`Its first language pair, Chinese to English, handled about 18 million translations a day (2016).`]},"tech:resnet":{intro:`A residual network adds shortcut connections that let a signal skip past a couple of layers and be added back in. Each block then only has to learn a small correction to its input rather than a whole new transformation. That simple change let networks grow from about twenty layers to more than a hundred and fifty and keep improving.`,how:`Without shortcuts, very deep networks got worse even on their own training data, because the error signal faded as it passed back through dozens of layers. In a residual block the output equals the input plus whatever the block computes, so a block that learns nothing simply passes its input through. Gradients flow back along the shortcuts untouched, which makes extra depth nearly free to add.`,history:`In December 2015 Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren and Jian Sun of Microsoft Research Asia in Beijing won the ImageNet challenge with a 152-layer ResNet. Its top-5 error of 3.57% in 2015 beat the 5.1% that Andrej Karpathy had scored when he labelled test images himself in 2014. In 2015 the same networks also won the COCO object detection and segmentation contests. In June 2016 the paper won the best paper award at the CVPR conference in Las Vegas. In 2017 the Transformer used the same shortcut connections around every one of its layers.`,facts:[`The winning ResNet had 152 layers, eight times deeper than the VGG networks of 2014.`,`The team also trained a 1,202-layer network on the small CIFAR-10 dataset (2015).`,`Its top-5 ImageNet error was 3.57% (2015).`]},"tech:rl":{intro:`Reinforcement learning teaches by trial and reward rather than by labelled examples: an agent acts, sees what happens and gets points for good outcomes. Deep reinforcement learning puts a deep neural network in charge of judging situations, so the agent can learn straight from raw inputs such as the pixels of a video game screen.`,how:`In DeepMind's Deep Q-Network, a convolutional network looks at the last four frames of an Atari game and estimates the future score of each joystick move. The agent mostly picks the best-looking move and sometimes a random one, to explore. It stores past moves in a replay memory and retrains on random samples of them, which keeps learning stable. The same program, with the same settings, learns dozens of different games.`,history:`In December 2013 DeepMind, a small London start-up, posted a paper showing one network learning seven Atari 2600 games from the screen alone. In January 2014 Google bought DeepMind for a reported 400 million pounds. In February 2015 the journal Nature published the full Deep Q-Network, which played 49 games and reached at least three quarters of a professional tester's score on 29 of them. In March 2016 AlphaGo, trained partly by playing against itself, beat the Go champion Lee Sedol by four games to one in Seoul. In October 2017 AlphaGo Zero learned Go from scratch, with no human games at all, and beat the version that had defeated Lee by 100 games to 0.`,facts:[`Over 200 million people watched the AlphaGo match (2016).`,`Google paid a reported 400 million pounds for DeepMind (2014).`,`AlphaGo Zero beat its predecessor 100 games to 0 after 3 days of self-play (2017).`]},"tech:transformer":{intro:`The Transformer is a neural network design built entirely on attention, with no recurrent loop reading words one at a time. Every word in a passage looks at every other word in a single step, which suits the parallel hardware of GPUs. It was introduced for translation, where it beat the best existing systems while training in a fraction of the time.`,how:`Each word becomes a vector, plus a signal for its position. In each layer every word makes a “query”, a “key” and a “value”; matching queries against keys decides how much each word borrows from the others, and several such attention “heads” run side by side. A small feed-forward network then processes each word, with shortcuts and normalisation around both steps. Stacking six or more such layers builds rich context.`,history:`In June 2017 eight Google researchers, among them Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, Jakob Uszkoreit and Llion Jones, posted “Attention Is All You Need”. In 2017 their big model scored 28.4 BLEU on English to German translation, beating every earlier system, after 3.5 days on eight NVIDIA P100 GPUs. In December 2017 the paper was presented at the NIPS conference in Long Beach, California. In 2018 the design became the basis of OpenAI's GPT and Google's BERT. In October 2020 Google's Vision Transformer showed the same design could classify images by treating patches as words.`,facts:[`The big Transformer trained for 3.5 days on 8 P100 GPUs (2017).`,`It scored a record 41.8 BLEU on English to French (2017).`,`All eight authors were listed as equal contributors (2017).`]},"tech:pretrain":{intro:`Generative pre-training teaches a language model in two stages. First it reads a huge amount of unlabelled text, learning to predict the next word, which forces it to absorb grammar, facts and some reasoning. Then a short round of fine-tuning on a small labelled dataset adapts the same model to a particular task, such as classifying reviews or answering questions.`,how:`Predicting the next word needs no human labels: the text itself is the answer key, so any book or web page becomes training data. A Transformer reads the text and outputs a probability for every possible next word, and the error adjusts its weights. After pre-training, a small extra layer and a little labelled data turn the general model into a specialist, needing far fewer examples than training from scratch.`,history:`In 2015 Andrew Dai and Quoc Le at Google showed that pre-training a recurrent network on unlabelled text improved later classification. In January 2018 Jeremy Howard and Sebastian Ruder's ULMFiT showed the recipe working well for text classification. In June 2018 OpenAI's GPT, a Transformer trained on about 7,000 unpublished books, improved the state of the art on 9 of the 12 tasks it was tested on. In October 2018 Google's BERT, which learned by filling in hidden words, swept the GLUE language benchmark. In February 2019 OpenAI's GPT-2 wrote fluent paragraphs, and OpenAI at first withheld the full model, citing fears of misuse.`,facts:[`GPT-1 had 117 million parameters and read about 7,000 books (2018).`,`BERT-Large had 340 million parameters (2018).`,`GPT-2's full 1.5-billion-parameter model was released in November 2019.`]},"tech:scaling":{intro:`Scaling laws are the discovery that a language model's error falls along a smooth, predictable curve as you add parameters, data and computing power. Plotted on logarithmic axes, the curves are nearly straight lines across many orders of magnitude. A lab can train a series of small models cheaply and forecast how well a giant one will do before spending millions on it.`,how:`Researchers train dozens of models of different sizes and measure their “loss”, the average surprise at the next word. Loss follows a power law: each tenfold increase in compute cuts it by a roughly constant fraction, as long as neither model size nor data is the bottleneck. The fitted curve says how to split a budget between a bigger model and more training text, and how much better the next model should be.`,history:`In December 2017 Joel Hestness and colleagues at Baidu found power-law learning curves in translation, speech and image models. In January 2020 Jared Kaplan, Sam McCandlish and others at OpenAI published “Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models”, fitting curves across more than seven orders of magnitude of compute. In May 2020 OpenAI's 175-billion-parameter GPT-3 landed on the same smooth curve. In March 2022 DeepMind's Chinchilla paper revised the recipe, finding that models should read far more data. In March 2023 OpenAI said it had predicted GPT-4's final loss from models trained with up to 10,000 times less compute.`,facts:[`Kaplan's curves spanned more than seven orders of magnitude of compute (2020).`,`GPT-3 took about 3.14×10²³ arithmetic operations to train (2020).`,`GPT-4's loss was forecast from runs using 10,000 times less compute (2023).`]},"tech:chinchilla":{intro:`Compute-optimal training answers a practical question: with a fixed budget of computing power, how big should a model be and how much text should it read? The answer, found at DeepMind, was that most large models were far too big for the data they saw. A smaller model fed several times more text gave a better result for the same cost.`,how:`Training cost grows with parameters times tokens read. The researchers trained over 400 models, from 70 million to over 16 billion parameters, on 5 to 500 billion tokens, and fitted which mix gave the lowest loss for each budget. Parameters and tokens should grow in equal proportion, about 20 tokens for every parameter. A smaller model has a bonus: it is cheaper to run for every user afterwards.`,history:`In 2020 OpenAI's scaling-law paper advised spending most extra compute on bigger models, and GPT-3 read only about 300 billion tokens for its 175 billion parameters. In December 2021 DeepMind's Gopher had 280 billion parameters, also trained on 300 billion tokens. In March 2022 Jordan Hoffmann and colleagues at DeepMind trained Chinchilla, 70 billion parameters on 1.4 trillion tokens, with the same compute as Gopher, and it beat Gopher on almost every benchmark. In February 2023 Meta's LLaMA went further on purpose, training a 7-billion-parameter model on a trillion tokens so it would be cheap to run. In 2024 Meta's Llama 3 8B read 15 trillion tokens, nearly 1,900 per parameter.`,facts:[`Chinchilla read nearly five times as much text as Gopher (2022).`,`Chinchilla scored 67.5% on the MMLU knowledge test, against Gopher's 60% (2022).`,`GPT-3 read under 2 tokens per parameter (2020).`]},"tech:datafilter":{intro:`Data curation is the craft of choosing what a model reads. The raw web is mostly junk: menus, spam, machine-written pages, cookie banners and endless copies of the same text. Filtering, cleaning and removing duplicates means each token of training teaches the model something, so a clean dataset can beat a much larger dirty one.`,how:`Pipelines strip page boilerplate, keep text in the wanted languages, and drop pages with too little real prose or too many bad words. Near-duplicate detection uses “MinHash” fingerprints to find pages that differ by only a few words. Classifiers trained to recognise good writing, such as Wikipedia articles or textbooks, score the rest, and only the best is kept. Mixing ratios decide how much code, books, science and dialogue go into the final blend.`,history:`In 2007 Gil Elbaz founded Common Crawl, a non-profit that publishes free copies of the web for anyone to study. In October 2019 Google's T5 paper released C4, the Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus, cleaned from one Common Crawl snapshot. In December 2020 the volunteer group EleutherAI released The Pile, drawn from 22 sources including books, code, scientific papers and court records. In July 2021 Katherine Lee and colleagues at Google showed that removing duplicates made models memorise less and learn more efficiently. In 2024 Hugging Face released FineWeb, built from 96 Common Crawl snapshots, and FineWeb-Edu, filtered by a classifier for educational value.`,facts:[`C4 held about 750 GB of cleaned English text (2019).`,`The Pile combined 22 sources into 825 GB (2020).`,`FineWeb held 15 trillion tokens (2024).`]},"tech:moe":{intro:`A mixture-of-experts model replaces one big block of neurons with many smaller “expert” blocks and a router that sends each word to just a few of them. The model can hold an enormous amount of knowledge in its total parameters while doing only a small model's arithmetic for each token. It is a way to get bigger without getting proportionally slower.`,how:`In each Transformer layer the feed-forward part is split into, say, 8 or 64 experts. A small router network scores the experts for each token and picks the top one or two, blending their outputs by its scores. Training must keep the load balanced, or a few popular experts do all the work while others starve. All the experts must still sit in memory, so the model needs many chips even though each token touches few weights.`,history:`In 1991 Robert Jacobs, Michael Jordan, Steven Nowlan and Geoffrey Hinton proposed “adaptive mixtures of local experts”. In January 2017 Noam Shazeer and colleagues at Google built a sparsely gated layer with thousands of experts and up to 137 billion parameters. In January 2021 Google's Switch Transformer routed each token to a single expert and reached 1.6 trillion parameters. In December 2023 the Paris start-up Mistral released Mixtral 8x7B with open weights; it matched much larger dense models. In December 2024 China's DeepSeek released V3, a 671-billion-parameter mixture of experts, trained for a reported 2.8 million GPU hours.`,facts:[`Switch Transformer had 1.6 trillion parameters (2021).`,`Mixtral used about 13 billion of its 47 billion parameters per token (2023).`,`DeepSeek-V3 activated 37 billion of 671 billion parameters per token (2024).`]},"tech:flash":{intro:`FlashAttention is a way of computing attention, the heart of a Transformer, that gives exactly the same answer as the textbook method but runs several times faster and uses far less memory. Its trick is not new mathematics but careful bookkeeping: it keeps the work inside the GPU's small, fast on-chip memory instead of shuttling huge tables to and from main memory.`,how:`Standard attention builds a table scoring every word against every other, millions of entries for a long passage, writes it out to the GPU's high-bandwidth memory and reads it back. FlashAttention cuts the inputs into tiles that fit in on-chip SRAM, computes each tile's share, and keeps a running maximum and sum so the softmax can be corrected as it goes. The full table is never stored, so memory grows with the length, not with its square.`,history:`In 2018 Maxim Milakov and Natalia Gimelshein at NVIDIA described the “online softmax” trick for computing a softmax in a single pass. In May 2022 Tri Dao, Dan Fu, Stefano Ermon, Atri Rudra and Christopher Ré at Stanford released FlashAttention, speeding up GPT-2 training about threefold. In March 2023 PyTorch 2.0 built it into its standard attention function. In July 2023 FlashAttention-2 roughly doubled the speed again by dividing the work better across the GPU. In July 2024 FlashAttention-3 used the H100's asynchronous copy engines and FP8 numbers to reach about three quarters of the chip's peak.`,facts:[`FlashAttention trained GPT-2 about three times faster than standard code (2022).`,`FlashAttention-3 reached about 740 teraflops on an H100, 75% of peak (2024).`]},"tech:optim":{intro:`An optimiser is the rule that decides how to change a network's weights after each batch of examples. For a decade almost everyone used Adam and its close variant AdamW. Newer optimisers treat each layer's weights as a matrix, not a bag of separate numbers, and reach the same quality with noticeably less compute.`,how:`Adam adjusts each weight on its own, scaling its step by how noisy that weight's gradient has been. Muon instead takes the momentum for a whole weight matrix and “orthogonalises” it with a few cheap Newton-Schulz iterations, so the update pushes equally in every direction rather than mostly along a few dominant ones. Paired with tricks that stop attention scores from exploding, it trains large models stably in fewer steps.`,history:`In 2014 Diederik Kingma and Jimmy Ba published Adam, and in 2017 Ilya Loshchilov and Frank Hutter's AdamW fixed how it handled weight decay. In 2018 Vineet Gupta, Tomer Koren and Yoram Singer at Google proposed Shampoo, an optimiser that also treats weights as matrices. In late 2024 Keller Jordan and collaborators introduced Muon while setting speed records for training small GPT-2 models. In February 2025 Moonshot AI in Beijing reported that Muon needed about half the compute of AdamW at larger scale. In July 2025 Moonshot trained Kimi K2, a trillion-parameter mixture-of-experts model, with its MuonClip variant without a single loss spike.`,facts:[`Moonshot found Muon about twice as compute-efficient as AdamW (2025).`,`Kimi K2 had a trillion parameters and read 15.5 trillion tokens (2025).`]},"tech:longctx":{intro:`A model's context window is how much text it can take in at once: the prompt, any documents, and its own reply. Early chatbots held a few pages before losing the start of the conversation. Long-context models read hundreds of thousands of words in one go, enough for a novel, a year of company reports or a whole codebase, and answer questions that need all of it.`,how:`Attention's cost grows with the square of the length, so long windows need efficient kernels such as FlashAttention and careful handling of the “KV cache” that stores every earlier word's keys and values. Position signals such as rotary embeddings are stretched so a model trained on short texts can handle longer ones, then it is fine-tuned on long documents. Tests hide a “needle” fact in a haystack of text to check the model really reads it all.`,history:`In 2018 GPT-1 read 512 tokens at a time, and in 2020 GPT-3 read 2,048. In March 2023 GPT-4 offered 8,000 tokens, with a 32,000-token version. In May 2023 Anthropic expanded Claude to 100,000 tokens, about 75,000 words, and showed it spotting one changed line in The Great Gatsby in 22 seconds. In November 2023 GPT-4 Turbo offered 128,000 tokens and Claude 2.1 offered 200,000. In February 2024 Google's Gemini 1.5 Pro handled up to a million tokens for users and 10 million in research tests.`,facts:[`Claude's 100,000-token window held about 75,000 words (2023).`,`Gemini 1.5 Pro found hidden facts in up to 10 million tokens in tests (2024).`,`GPT-3's context was 2,048 tokens (2020).`]},"tech:multimodal":{intro:`A multimodal model handles more than one kind of input, usually text and images and sometimes audio and video, inside a single network. Instead of separate programs for reading, seeing and listening, one model can look at a photo of a broken bicycle, a chart or a handwritten note and talk about it in plain language.`,how:`An image is cut into patches, and a vision encoder turns each patch into a vector, much as words become vectors; a small adapter maps these into the language model's space so they sit in the prompt like extra words. Some models are trained on mixed text and images from the start. Training data pairs pictures with captions, documents with their text, and questions with answers about images.`,history:`In January 2021 OpenAI's CLIP learned to match images with captions from picture and text pairs collected from the web. In April 2022 DeepMind's Flamingo answered questions about images after seeing just a few examples. In September 2023 OpenAI released GPT-4 with vision, GPT-4V, to ChatGPT users. In December 2023 Google introduced Gemini, trained from the start on text, images, audio and video. In May 2024 OpenAI's GPT-4o took text, pictures and sound into one network and could answer aloud in about a third of a second.`,facts:[`CLIP trained on 400 million image and caption pairs (2021).`,`GPT-4o answered spoken questions in about 320 milliseconds on average (2024).`]},"tech:synthetic":{intro:`Synthetic data is training material written by AI models rather than by people. As the supply of good human text runs short, labs have strong models write explanations, worked problems, code and conversations for new models to learn from. Done carelessly it spreads the teacher's mistakes; done well, with each example checked, it turns spare computing power into fresh lessons.`,how:`A strong model is prompted with topics, difficulty levels and personas to produce varied textbook-style passages or exercises. Answers that can be checked are verified: code is run against tests, maths answers are compared with known results, and other models grade the rest. Only the passing examples are kept. Variety matters as much as correctness: thousands of near-identical stories teach little.`,history:`In December 2022 Yizhong Wang and colleagues at the University of Washington showed with Self-Instruct that a model could write its own instruction-following examples. In March 2023 Stanford's Alpaca fine-tuned Meta's LLaMA on 52,000 examples written by an OpenAI model. In May 2023 Ronen Eldan and Yuanzhi Li's TinyStories showed tiny models learning fluent English from simple machine-written stories. In June 2023 Microsoft's Phi-1, trained largely on “textbook quality” data written by GPT-3.5, beat much bigger models on Python tests. In July 2024 a Nature paper led by Ilia Shumailov warned that models trained repeatedly on their own output “collapse”, losing rare knowledge. In 2024 Meta used Llama models to generate and check much of Llama 3's fine-tuning data.`,facts:[`Alpaca's 52,000 machine-written examples cost under 600 dollars to make (2023).`,`Phi-1 scored 50.6% on the HumanEval coding test with only 1.3 billion parameters (2023).`]},"tech:gans":{intro:`A generative adversarial network, or GAN, pits two neural networks against each other. The generator turns random numbers into fake images; the discriminator sees a mix of real and fake images and tries to tell them apart. Each improves by beating the other, until the generator's pictures are hard to distinguish from real photographs.`,how:`The discriminator is trained to say “real” for training images and “fake” for generated ones. The generator is trained through the discriminator's judgement, nudging its weights so its fakes score as more real. Neither network is ever given a rule for what a face or a bedroom looks like. Training is a delicate balance: if one side wins too easily the other stops learning, and generators often “collapse” into producing only a few kinds of image.`,history:`In June 2014 Ian Goodfellow, a PhD student at the University of Montreal, published GANs with Yoshua Bengio and others; the idea reportedly came to him after a discussion with friends in a bar. In November 2015 Alec Radford's DCGAN used convolutional layers to make GANs train stably on bedrooms and faces. In October 2017 NVIDIA's progressive GAN generated celebrity-like faces at 1024 by 1024 pixels. In October 2018 a GAN portrait, Edmond de Belamy, sold at Christie's in New York. In December 2018 NVIDIA's StyleGAN made photorealistic portraits of people who never existed, and in February 2019 the website This Person Does Not Exist showed a new one on every visit.`,facts:[`The GAN portrait sold at Christie's for 432,500 dollars (2018).`,`StyleGAN's faces were 1024 by 1024 pixels (2018).`]},"tech:diffusion":{intro:`A diffusion model generates images by learning to remove noise. During training it watches pictures being slowly buried under random static and learns to undo each small step. To create something new it starts from pure static and cleans it up step by step until a recognisable picture emerges, steered by a text description if one is given.`,how:`Training adds a random amount of noise to an image and asks a network, usually a U-Net, to predict the noise that was added. Generation runs this in reverse over dozens or hundreds of steps, each subtracting a little of the predicted noise. To follow a prompt the network also sees an encoding of the text, and “classifier-free guidance” exaggerates the difference between its prompted and unprompted predictions. Working in a compressed “latent” space instead of on pixels makes it far cheaper.`,history:`In 2015 Jascha Sohl-Dickstein and colleagues at Stanford proposed diffusion models, borrowing ideas from thermodynamics. In June 2020 Jonathan Ho, Ajay Jain and Pieter Abbeel at Berkeley published denoising diffusion probabilistic models, DDPM, with images as good as GANs. In May 2021 Prafulla Dhariwal and Alex Nichol at OpenAI showed diffusion beating GANs on ImageNet. In April 2022 OpenAI's DALL-E 2 and in May 2022 Google's Imagen made striking images from text. In August 2022 Stability AI released Stable Diffusion, developed with the CompVis group at LMU Munich and Runway, as open weights that ran on a home graphics card.`,facts:[`DDPM used 1,000 small noising steps (2020).`,`Stable Diffusion ran in under 10 GB of graphics memory (2022).`]},"tech:dit":{intro:`A diffusion transformer swaps the convolutional U-Net at the heart of an image diffusion model for a Transformer, the design behind language models. The picture is cut into small patches that are treated like words. Because Transformers improve predictably as they grow, image and video models can then be scaled up with the same recipes that make chatbots better.`,how:`The image, compressed into a latent grid, is cut into patches of a few latent pixels each, and each patch becomes a token. Transformer layers let every patch attend to every other, while the noise level and the prompt adjust each layer's normalisation. For video, patches extend through time as well as space, so one model learns both how things look and how they move. Bigger models given more compute make steadily better pictures.`,history:`In October 2020 Google's Vision Transformer showed that images cut into 16 by 16 pixel patches could be handled like words. In December 2022 William Peebles at Berkeley and Saining Xie at New York University published “Scalable Diffusion Models with Transformers”, whose DiT models improved steadily with more compute and set a record on ImageNet. In February 2024 OpenAI previewed Sora, a diffusion transformer that generated up to a minute of video; Peebles co-led the project. In February 2024 Stability AI announced Stable Diffusion 3, built on a multimodal diffusion transformer. In August 2024 Black Forest Labs, founded by Stable Diffusion's original authors, released its Flux image models on the same principle.`,facts:[`DiT-XL/2 scored a record FID of 2.27 on ImageNet, where lower means more realistic (2022).`,`Sora's preview clips ran up to 60 seconds (2024).`]},"tech:video":{intro:`Video generation models produce moving pictures from a sentence or a still image: a dog surfing at sunset, a drone shot over a city that never existed. They must keep objects, faces and lighting consistent from frame to frame and make motion look natural, a far harder job than a single picture. Physics is learned only from watching, so glasses sometimes pass through tables.`,how:`Most use a diffusion transformer that works on a compressed block of video cut into spacetime patches. Starting from noise, the model cleans up all the frames together, so the whole clip is planned at once rather than frame by frame. Training needs millions of captioned clips and huge amounts of computing, and each second of output costs far more than a single image. The newest models generate matching sound as well.`,history:`In 2022 Meta's Make-A-Video and Google's Imagen Video produced short, blurry clips from text. In 2023 Runway's Gen-2 became one of the first text-to-video tools the public could use. In February 2024 OpenAI showed Sora making clips up to a minute long, and it opened Sora to ChatGPT subscribers in December 2024. In June 2024 the Chinese video app company Kuaishou released Kling. In May 2025 Google's Veo 3 generated video with synchronised speech, music and sound effects. In September 2025 OpenAI launched the Sora app, a social feed of generated videos.`,facts:[`Sora's first public version made clips of up to 20 seconds at 1080p (2024).`,`The Sora app passed a million downloads in under five days (2025).`]},"tech:neuraltts":{intro:`Neural speech synthesis makes a computer talk by having a neural network generate the sound wave itself, instead of stitching together recorded snippets or driving a buzzing vocoder. The result has natural breathing, rhythm and intonation. A voice assistant built this way sounds like a person reading aloud, not a robot announcing train times.`,how:`WaveNet predicts each audio sample, one of 16,000 a second, from the samples before it, using stacks of “dilated” convolutions that look back over longer and longer stretches of sound. It is conditioned on the sounds of the text and on the speaker's identity, so one model can speak in many voices. Generating one sample at a time made the first version far too slow for real-time use.`,history:`In September 2016 DeepMind unveiled WaveNet, which listeners rated as closing more than half the gap between the best existing systems and real human speech. In October 2017 a version a thousand times faster began speaking for Google Assistant. In December 2017 Google's Tacotron 2 paired a network that turned text into a spectrogram with a WaveNet that turned the spectrogram into sound, and listeners rated it almost as natural as recordings. In January 2023 the start-up ElevenLabs opened its voice-cloning tool to the public, and within days it was misused to fake celebrities' voices.`,facts:[`Tacotron 2 scored 4.53 out of 5 for naturalness, against 4.58 for real recordings (2017).`,`Parallel WaveNet ran 1,000 times faster than the original (2017).`]},"tech:stt":{intro:`Robust speech recognition turns messy real-world audio into accurate text: accents, background noise, mumbling, technical words and dozens of languages. Older systems worked well on clean recordings like those they were trained on and stumbled on anything else. Training one large Transformer on an enormous, varied pile of audio from the web made transcription work almost anywhere, without fine-tuning.`,how:`The audio is cut into 30-second windows and turned into a log-Mel spectrogram, a picture of which frequencies are loud at each moment. An encoder reads the picture and a decoder writes out the text token by token, with special tokens for the language, timestamps and whether to translate into English. Because the training transcripts came from the web, the model learned punctuation and capital letters too.`,history:`In 2012 Geoffrey Hinton and colleagues from Microsoft, Google, IBM and Toronto reported that deep neural networks had sharply cut speech recognition errors at all four groups. In October 2016 Microsoft Research reported matching professional transcribers on the Switchboard telephone test. In June 2020 Facebook's wav2vec 2.0 learned speech from unlabelled audio. In September 2022 OpenAI released Whisper as open source, trained on web audio in nearly 100 languages. In March 2023 OpenAI offered Whisper through its API at 0.6 cents a minute.`,facts:[`Whisper trained on 680,000 hours of audio, about 77 years of listening (2022).`,`Microsoft's system made 5.9% word errors on telephone speech, like human transcribers (2016).`]},"tech:musicgen":{intro:`Music generation models compose and perform songs from a text description, such as a melancholy folk song about leaving home, with fiddle. The best write lyrics, sing them, and produce verses, choruses and instrumental breaks in a polished mix. They raise hard questions for musicians and record labels about what the models learned from and who owns the result.`,how:`An audio codec network compresses sound into a stream of discrete tokens, a few dozen per second at several levels of detail. A Transformer then generates these tokens one after another, just as a language model writes words, conditioned on the text prompt or a hummed melody. The codec's decoder turns the tokens back into a waveform. Long-range structure, such as a chorus that returns, is the hardest part to get right.`,history:`In April 2020 OpenAI's Jukebox generated raw-audio songs in the style of famous artists, but was very slow to render. In January 2023 Google described MusicLM, which made music from text descriptions. In June 2023 Meta released MusicGen as open source, trained only on licensed music. In 2024 the start-ups Suno and Udio launched apps that made full songs with vocals in seconds. In June 2024 the major record labels, through the RIAA, sued both for copyright infringement. In late 2025 Universal Music settled with Udio and Warner Music with both Udio and Suno, turning the lawsuits into licensing deals.`,facts:[`Jukebox took about nine hours to render one minute of music (2020).`,`MusicGen trained on 20,000 hours of licensed music (2023).`]},"tech:worldmodel":{intro:`An interactive world model is a video generator you can steer. Instead of producing a fixed clip, it generates the next frames in response to your actions: walk forward, turn left, open the door, and it imagines what you would see, keeping the room the same when you look back. It is a playable dream, useful for games, for training robots and for simulating rare road situations.`,how:`The model is trained on huge amounts of video, often paired with the actions that caused each change, such as controller inputs or steering. At each step it takes the frames so far plus the newest action and predicts the next frame, fast enough to run live. Keeping the world consistent for minutes means remembering what lies out of view. Text prompts can add events, such as rain or a herd of deer.`,history:`In March 2018 David Ha and Jürgen Schmidhuber's “World Models” paper trained a car-racing agent inside a network's own dream of the game. In 2023 the London start-up Wayve's GAIA-1 generated realistic driving videos that responded to steering and speed. In February 2024 Google DeepMind's Genie turned single images into playable two-dimensional platform games. In August 2024 Google researchers' GameNGen ran the 1993 game Doom entirely inside a neural network. In January 2025 NVIDIA released its Cosmos world models for robots and cars. In August 2025 Google DeepMind's Genie 3 generated explorable 3D worlds at 720p and 24 frames a second that stayed consistent for a few minutes.`,facts:[`GameNGen ran Doom at 20 frames a second on a single TPU (2024).`,`NVIDIA's Cosmos models learned from 20 million hours of video (2025).`]},"tech:jepa":{intro:`A joint-embedding predictive architecture, or JEPA, learns about the world by predicting what will happen in an abstract summary space, rather than predicting every pixel or every word. Its champion, Yann LeCun, argues that this is closer to how animals learn: a cat does not imagine each leaf on a tree, only that the branch will move. Such a model would learn common sense from video, and plan.`,how:`Two encoders turn the visible part of an input and a hidden part, such as a masked patch of an image or the next second of a video, into compact representations. A predictor learns to guess the hidden part's representation from the visible part's. Because errors are measured in this abstract space, the model can ignore unpredictable detail such as the exact pattern of ripples on water. A JEPA trained on video could plan actions by imagining their outcomes.`,history:`In June 2022 Yann LeCun, Meta's chief AI scientist, published “A Path Towards Autonomous Machine Intelligence”, setting out the JEPA idea. In June 2023 Meta released I-JEPA, which learned image features by predicting masked regions. In February 2024 Meta's V-JEPA applied the method to video. In June 2025 V-JEPA 2 was used to plan simple robot arm movements in rooms it had never seen. In late 2025 LeCun left Meta to found a start-up, Advanced Machine Intelligence Labs, to pursue such world models. Forecasters expected JEPA-style world models to come of age around 2027.`,facts:[`V-JEPA 2 learned from more than a million hours of internet video (2025).`,`LeCun shared the 2018 Turing Award with Geoffrey Hinton and Yoshua Bengio.`]},"tech:continual":{intro:`Continual learning would let an AI model keep learning after it is released, picking up new facts, skills and a user's preferences from experience, the way a new employee improves on the job. Language models are frozen at their training cut-off: they forget a conversation when it ends and know nothing that happened after their data was gathered, unless it is pasted into the prompt.`,how:`The obstacle is “catastrophic forgetting”: training a network on new material tends to overwrite what it already knew. Proposed remedies include protecting the weights most important to old skills, replaying a mix of old data, adding small new modules for new knowledge, and giving models long-term memories they can write to and search. A working system would also have to decide what is worth remembering and resist being taught falsehoods.`,history:`In 1989 Michael McCloskey and Neal Cohen described catastrophic interference: neural networks trained on a new task forgot the old one. In 2017 James Kirkpatrick and colleagues at DeepMind proposed elastic weight consolidation, which slows changes to the weights that mattered most for earlier tasks. In 2024 ChatGPT added a memory feature that stored notes about users between conversations, and in 2025 Claude gained a similar one: workarounds rather than true learning. By 2026 continual learning was widely named as one of the main missing pieces on the way to more general AI. Forecasters expected models that learn on the job around 2028.`,facts:[`Elastic weight consolidation let one network learn several Atari games in turn (2017).`,`ChatGPT's memory feature began testing in February 2024.`]},"tech:rsi":{intro:`Automated AI research would mean AI systems doing the work of AI scientists: proposing ideas, writing the code for experiments, running them on clusters, reading the results and deciding what to try next. Because better AI would then speed up the making of better AI, some researchers expect a feedback loop that could make progress very fast, which is why it worries safety researchers most of all.`,how:`An agent would combine strong coding skills, a long memory and access to a cluster. Given a research goal, it would read the literature, form hypotheses, implement them as training runs, analyse the curves and write up what worked, with many copies working in parallel day and night. The hard parts are taste in choosing problems, noticing subtle bugs, and judging results honestly rather than gaming the metric.`,history:`In 1965 the statistician I J Good wrote that an “ultraintelligent machine” could design even better machines, setting off an “intelligence explosion”. In August 2024 the Tokyo start-up Sakana AI's “AI Scientist” generated ideas, ran experiments and wrote complete machine learning papers. In October 2024 OpenAI's MLE-bench tested AI agents on real Kaggle data science competitions. In March 2025 a paper written by Sakana's system passed peer review at a workshop of the ICLR conference. In April 2025 Daniel Kokotajlo and colleagues published a widely read scenario in which AI agents take over most AI research within a few years. Forecasters expected AI to automate much of AI research around 2029.`,facts:[`Sakana's AI Scientist produced a full paper for about 15 dollars (2024).`,`MLE-bench set agents 75 real Kaggle competitions (2024).`]},"tech:distill":{intro:`Knowledge distillation trains a small “student” network to imitate a large “teacher” network, or a whole committee of them. The student learns not just the teacher's final answers but how confident it was in every alternative, which carries far more information than a bare label. The result is a model small and fast enough to run cheaply that keeps much of the big one's skill.`,how:`The teacher's output probabilities are “softened” with a temperature setting so that small probabilities become visible: a handwritten 2 might be judged a little like a 3 and a little like a 7. The student is trained to match these soft targets as well as the true labels. Hinton called these hints “dark knowledge”, because they reveal how the teacher sees the similarities between classes.`,history:`In 2006 Cristian Bucila, Rich Caruana and Alexandru Niculescu-Mizil showed that a large ensemble of models could be compressed into one small network trained on the ensemble's outputs. In 2014 Jimmy Ba and Rich Caruana trained shallow networks to mimic deep ones and match much of their accuracy. In March 2015 Geoffrey Hinton, Oriol Vinyals and Jeff Dean at Google published “Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network”, naming the method. In the same 2015 paper they distilled an ensemble of speech models from Android voice search into a single model with nearly the same accuracy. In October 2019 Hugging Face's DistilBERT kept almost all of BERT's language understanding in a much smaller, faster model.`,facts:[`DistilBERT kept 97% of BERT's skill with 40% fewer parameters (2019).`,`DistilBERT ran 60% faster than BERT (2019).`]},"tech:distill2":{intro:`Modern distillation copies a large model's way of working, not just its answers. The big model writes out detailed reasoning, explanations and full probability distributions for huge numbers of prompts, and a much smaller model is trained on all of it. Small, cheap models trained this way handle maths and coding problems that only giant models could manage a year or two before.`,how:`There are two main flavours. In one, the teacher generates long step-by-step solutions, wrong ones are filtered out, and the student is fine-tuned on the rest. In the other, the student is trained to match the teacher's probability for every possible next token, a far richer signal than the single word that actually came next. Both let a lab spend its giant model's compute once and sell a cheaper model many times over.`,history:`In June 2023 Microsoft's Orca trained a 13-billion-parameter model on step-by-step explanations from GPT-4 rather than bare answers. In May 2024 Google said its Gemini 1.5 Flash had been distilled from the larger Gemini 1.5 Pro. In June 2024 Google's open Gemma 2 models were trained to match a bigger teacher's token probabilities. In January 2025 DeepSeek released six small models distilled from the reasoning of its R1 model, and the 32-billion-parameter one beat OpenAI's o1-mini on several maths tests. In January 2025 OpenAI said it had seen signs that DeepSeek had distilled from its models, which its terms of use forbade.`,facts:[`DeepSeek's distilled models learned from about 800,000 solutions written by R1 (2025).`,`Orca learned from about 5 million ChatGPT and 1 million GPT-4 explanations (2023).`]},"tech:rlhf":{intro:`Reinforcement learning from human feedback, or RLHF, teaches a language model what people actually want. A model pre-trained on the internet can continue any text, but it is just as happy to ramble, make things up or be rude. With RLHF, people compare pairs of its answers, and the model is trained to produce the kind people prefer: helpful, honest and polite.`,how:`First the model is fine-tuned on examples of good answers written by people. Then labellers rank several of its responses to the same prompt, and these rankings train a separate “reward model” that predicts which answer a person would pick. Finally reinforcement learning, usually an algorithm called PPO, adjusts the language model to score highly with the reward model, with a penalty for drifting too far from where it started.`,history:`In 2017 Paul Christiano at OpenAI and Jan Leike at DeepMind, with colleagues, showed that people comparing short video clips could teach a simulated robot to do a backflip. In 2020 OpenAI used the method to train better summarisers of Reddit posts. In January 2022 OpenAI made InstructGPT the default model in its API; people preferred its 1.3-billion-parameter version to the 175-billion-parameter GPT-3. In November 2022 OpenAI's ChatGPT was trained in the same way. In May 2023 Rafael Rafailov and colleagues at Stanford published Direct Preference Optimisation, which learns straight from preference pairs without a separate reward model.`,facts:[`The backflip took about 900 human comparisons, under an hour of a person's time (2017).`,`InstructGPT's rankings came from a team of about 40 contractors (2022).`]},"tech:cot":{intro:`Chain-of-thought prompting gets a language model to write out its reasoning step by step before giving an answer. Like a pupil showing their working, the model makes fewer mistakes on arithmetic, logic puzzles and word problems, because each intermediate result is written down where it can build on it. It needs no retraining, just a different way of asking.`,how:`A model produces text one token at a time, with a fixed amount of computation per token, so a hard problem answered in one word gets very little thought. Writing intermediate steps gives it more tokens, and so more computation, and lets it use its own earlier steps as a scratchpad. A few worked examples in the prompt, or simply the words “Let's think step by step”, trigger the behaviour. It mainly helps large models; small ones write confident nonsense.`,history:`In November 2021 Maxwell Nye and colleagues at Google showed that models trained to write intermediate steps on a “scratchpad” did better at arithmetic and at tracing programs. In January 2022 Jason Wei, Denny Zhou and colleagues at Google published chain-of-thought prompting, with eight worked examples in the prompt. In March 2022 Xuezhi Wang and colleagues' “self-consistency” sampled many chains and took a majority vote, doing better still. In May 2022 Takeshi Kojima and colleagues at the University of Tokyo and Google found that simply adding “Let's think step by step” helped too. In September 2024 OpenAI's o1 was trained to produce long chains of thought by itself.`,facts:[`With chain of thought, PaLM's score on school maths problems rose from 18% to 57% (2022).`,`One added sentence lifted a model's arithmetic score from 18% to 79% (2022).`]},"tech:cai":{intro:`Constitutional AI trains a model to be harmless using a short written list of principles, its “constitution”, instead of relying entirely on people labelling thousands of harmful answers. The model criticises and rewrites its own responses in light of the principles, and AI judgements about which answers follow them better replace much of the human feedback. The rules it follows are written down where anyone can read them.`,how:`In the first stage the model answers difficult prompts, is asked to critique its answer against a randomly chosen principle, such as avoiding anything dangerous or unethical, and then revises it; it is fine-tuned on the revisions. In the second stage the model compares pairs of answers against the principles, and those AI judgements train a preference model used for reinforcement learning. The aim is a model that stays helpful, explaining its objections instead of refusing evasively.`,history:`In 2021 former OpenAI staff led by Dario and Daniela Amodei founded Anthropic in San Francisco. In April 2022 Anthropic published research on training a helpful and harmless assistant with human feedback. In December 2022 Yuntao Bai and colleagues at Anthropic published “Constitutional AI: Harmlessness from AI Feedback”. In March 2023 Anthropic released Claude, trained with the method. In May 2023 Anthropic published Claude's constitution, drawing on sources including the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and Apple's terms of service. In October 2023 Anthropic and the Collective Intelligence Project tested a constitution drafted with input from members of the public.`,facts:[`About 1,000 Americans helped draft the public constitution in the experiment (2023).`,`The paper called its second stage RLAIF, reinforcement learning from AI feedback (2022).`]},"tech:reasoning":{intro:`Reasoning models are language models trained to think before they answer, sometimes for minutes, writing long private chains of thought in which they try approaches, check their work and back out of dead ends. They are trained by reinforcement learning on problems whose answers can be checked automatically, such as maths and programming, and they solve problems far beyond earlier chatbots.`,how:`The model attempts thousands of problems, each attempt a long chain of thought ending in an answer. A program checks the answer, a number against the known solution or code against unit tests, and the reward strengthens whatever reasoning led to correct results. Over training the chains grow longer and the model picks up habits nobody programmed, such as spotting its own mistakes. Letting it think longer when answering also raises accuracy, a second way to spend compute.`,history:`In March 2022 Eric Zelikman and colleagues at Stanford proposed STaR, which fine-tuned a model on its own reasoning chains that had reached correct answers. In September 2024 OpenAI previewed o1, which solved most problems from the AIME, a qualifying exam for the US Mathematical Olympiad, against about 13% for GPT-4o. In January 2025 China's DeepSeek released R1 with open weights and described its recipe, built on a simple reward for correct answers. In July 2025 experimental reasoning models from Google DeepMind and OpenAI reached gold-medal scores at the International Mathematical Olympiad.`,facts:[`o1 solved 83% of AIME problems when allowed to vote among many attempts (2024).`,`o1 placed in the 89th percentile of Codeforces programming contests (2024).`,`Both Olympiad gold-medal models scored 35 of a possible 42 points (2025).`]},"tech:agents":{intro:`An AI agent is a language model that does not just answer but acts: it searches the web, runs code, calls other programs, clicks around a computer screen and carries on through dozens or hundreds of steps toward a goal. Instead of writing one reply, it works in a loop of planning, acting, looking at the result and trying again until the task is done.`,how:`The model is given descriptions of tools, such as a search engine, a code interpreter or a file editor, and replies in a structured format when it wants to use one. The surrounding program carries out the call and pastes the result back into the model's context, and the loop continues. Reliability is the challenge: a small error rate per step compounds over a long task, so models are trained specifically to recover from mistakes and check their work.`,history:`In October 2022 Shunyu Yao and colleagues at Princeton and Google published ReAct, which interleaved reasoning with actions such as Wikipedia searches. In February 2023 Meta's Toolformer taught a model to call calculators and search engines by itself. In April 2023 the open-source AutoGPT, which chained GPT-4 calls toward a goal, became one of GitHub's fastest-growing projects. In June 2023 OpenAI added function calling to its API. In October 2024 Anthropic gave Claude “computer use”, letting it move a mouse and type on a virtual desktop by looking at screenshots. In November 2024 Anthropic introduced the Model Context Protocol, an open standard for connecting models to tools and data.`,facts:[`On the OSWorld computer test, Claude's first computer use scored 14.9%, against 7.8% for the next best (2024).`,`METR found the length of tasks agents could finish doubling about every seven months (2025).`]},"tech:system1":{intro:`A System One model is an AI built to decide rather than to chat. Given a situation and a fixed set of allowed answers, such as which team should handle a support ticket or whether a payment looks fraudulent, it scores every option at once and returns a calibrated probability for each, in a fraction of a second. The name borrows psychology's term for fast, intuitive thinking.`,how:`A chatbot writes its answer token by token, and every token costs time and money. A System One model reads the input once and, in a single parallel pass, assigns a probability to each allowed answer, so there is nothing to write out. Training rewards calibration: when it says 80%, it should be right about 80% of the time, so programs can act automatically on confident answers and pass doubtful ones to a person.`,history:`In 2011 the psychologist Daniel Kahneman's book Thinking, Fast and Slow popularised the split between fast, intuitive “System 1” and slow, deliberate “System 2” thinking. In 2017 Chuan Guo and colleagues at Cornell showed that modern deep networks were badly overconfident and proposed simple fixes for their calibration. By 2024 many companies used large language models to sort emails and route requests, paying for written answers to what were really multiple-choice decisions. In September 2026 TypeSafe AI launched Jev, which it called the first System One model, trained with a method it named reinforcement learning for calibrated decisions. At its launch in 2026 Jev was offered for routing, classification, scoring and checking, not for chat or writing code.`,facts:[`Jev answered in 70 to 500 milliseconds, and its output tokens were free (2026).`,`Kahneman won the Nobel prize in economics in 2002.`]},"tech:interp":{intro:`Interpretability research tries to understand what is going on inside a neural network: which internal signals stand for which concepts, and how they combine into decisions. A trained model is billions of numbers that nobody wrote by hand. Being able to read them would let engineers check whether a model is being deceptive, trace the source of a bias, or switch off a dangerous behaviour directly.`,how:`A single neuron rarely means one thing: models pack more concepts than they have neurons by spreading each concept over many, a “superposition”. Researchers train a “sparse autoencoder” on a model's internal activity to unpack it into many more features, each of which fires for one recognisable idea, such as a city, a bug in code or flattery. Turning a feature up or down and watching the model's behaviour change confirms what it does.`,history:`In November 2017 Chris Olah and colleagues at Google published “Feature Visualization”, showing what individual neurons in image networks responded to. In March 2021 Gabriel Goh, Olah and colleagues at OpenAI found a neuron in CLIP that responded to Spider-Man in photos, drawings and written text. In September 2022 Anthropic published “Toy Models of Superposition”, explaining why neurons mix concepts. In October 2023 Anthropic used sparse autoencoders to pull clear features out of a small model. In May 2024 it found millions of features inside Claude 3 Sonnet, and offered “Golden Gate Claude”, with the Golden Gate Bridge feature turned up so high the model thought it was the bridge. In March 2025 Anthropic traced circuits showing Claude planning rhyming words ahead when writing poetry.`,facts:[`Anthropic extracted about 34 million features from Claude 3 Sonnet (2024).`,`Golden Gate Claude was open to the public for 24 hours (2024).`]},"tech:quant8":{intro:`FP8 inference runs a trained AI model using 8-bit floating-point numbers instead of the usual 16-bit ones. Each weight and activation takes half the memory, twice as many pass through the chip's memory channels each second, and matrix units built for FP8 do twice the multiplications. Careful scaling keeps the answers almost identical to the full-precision model's.`,how:`An 8-bit float has a sign bit, a few exponent bits for range and a few mantissa bits for precision: the E4M3 format has four and three, E5M2 five and two. Because 8 bits cover only a narrow range, each tensor gets a scale factor, chosen from the largest values seen, that shifts its numbers into range. Sensitive parts can stay in higher precision. Serving is mostly limited by memory bandwidth, so halving the bytes nearly doubles the speed.`,history:`In October 2017 NVIDIA and Baidu researchers showed “mixed precision” training with 16-bit numbers, and in 2017 Google's second TPU made the 16-bit bfloat16 format common. In March 2022 NVIDIA announced the H100, with FP8 Tensor Cores and a “Transformer Engine” that managed the scaling automatically. In September 2022 NVIDIA, Arm and Intel published a joint proposal for the E4M3 and E5M2 formats. In 2023 NVIDIA's TensorRT-LLM software made FP8 serving routine on H100s. In December 2024 DeepSeek-V3 was trained largely in FP8, one of the first very large models to be.`,facts:[`An H100 does about 1,979 teraflops of dense FP8 maths, twice its 16-bit rate (2022).`,`An 8-bit number has just 256 possible values (2022).`]},"tech:quant4":{intro:`FP4 inference squeezes each number in a neural network into just four bits, so a value can take only 16 possible forms. It sounds too crude to keep a model's skill, but by grouping numbers into small blocks that share a scale factor, large models keep nearly all their quality while running twice as fast as with FP8, in a quarter of the memory of 16-bit numbers.`,how:`The FP4 format E2M1 has one sign bit, two exponent bits and one mantissa bit, giving values such as 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4 and 6, either positive or negative. NVIDIA's NVFP4 gives every block of 16 numbers its own 8-bit scale, plus one scale for the whole tensor, so each block is stretched to fit. Weights are converted after training, sometimes with a short fine-tune to recover accuracy, or trained at low precision from the start.`,history:`In August 2022 Tim Dettmers and colleagues showed that a few large “outlier” values were what broke simple 8-bit and 4-bit compression of language models. In May 2023 Dettmers' QLoRA fine-tuned a 65-billion-parameter model on a single GPU using 4-bit weights. In September 2023 the Open Compute Project published microscaling formats, including MXFP4, backed by AMD, Arm, Intel, Meta, Microsoft, NVIDIA and Qualcomm. In March 2024 NVIDIA announced Blackwell, the first GPU family with FP4 Tensor Cores. In August 2025 OpenAI released its open gpt-oss models with their expert weights already stored in MXFP4.`,facts:[`gpt-oss-120b fitted on a single 80 GB GPU thanks to its 4-bit weights (2025).`,`NVIDIA quoted Blackwell at up to 20 petaflops of FP4, counting sparsity (2024).`]},"tech:specdec":{intro:`Speculative decoding speeds up a large language model without changing a single word of what it would have written. A small, fast “draft” model guesses the next several words, and the large model checks all the guesses at once in one pass. Whenever the guesses are right, which on easy stretches of text is often, several words come out for the price of one.`,how:`Generating text is slow because each new token needs a full pass through the big model, which mostly waits for its weights to arrive from memory. Checking five drafted tokens takes about the same time as producing one, since the weights are read only once. The big model accepts the drafted tokens up to the first one it disagrees with, then supplies its own; a careful acceptance rule guarantees the output follows exactly the big model's probabilities.`,history:`In 2018 Mitchell Stern, Noam Shazeer and Jakob Uszkoreit at Google proposed “blockwise parallel decoding”, predicting several tokens ahead and verifying them. In November 2022 Yaniv Leviathan, Matan Kalman and Yossi Matias at Google published speculative decoding, with outputs identical to the big model's. In February 2023 Charlie Chen and colleagues at DeepMind independently described speculative sampling, speeding up the 70-billion-parameter Chinchilla two to two and a half times. In January 2024 the Medusa method added extra prediction heads to the big model itself instead of using a separate draft model. In December 2024 DeepSeek-V3 was trained to predict two tokens at a time and used the extra one for speculation.`,facts:[`Google's first tests sped up a T5 model two to three times (2022).`,`DeepSeek-V3's extra predicted token was accepted 85 to 90% of the time (2024).`]},"tech:batching":{intro:`Continuous batching serves a language model to many users at once on the same chips. Producing each word means reading all the model's weights from memory, which takes far longer than the arithmetic, so it pays to do the arithmetic for many conversations during a single read. The trick is letting requests join and leave the batch at every step, so no chip time is wasted waiting.`,how:`In simple batching, a group of requests starts together and the batch runs until the longest reply is finished, while completed slots sit idle. Continuous batching checks after every token: finished requests leave and new ones take their places. Each conversation's attention memory, the KV cache, grows unpredictably, so PagedAttention stores it in small fixed-size pages, like a computer's virtual memory, instead of reserving one big block per user.`,history:`In July 2022 Gyeong-In Yu and colleagues at Seoul National University described “iteration-level scheduling” in their Orca serving system. In April 2023 a team at UC Berkeley began using its new vLLM software to serve the Vicuna chatbot and the Chatbot Arena. In June 2023 Woosuk Kwon, Zhuohan Li and colleagues released vLLM as open source, with PagedAttention managing the memory. In 2023 Hugging Face's Text Generation Inference and NVIDIA's TensorRT-LLM added continuous batching too. In January 2024 SGLang, also from Berkeley, reused cached work for prompts that start the same way.`,facts:[`vLLM served up to 24 times more requests than Hugging Face's standard library (2023).`,`PagedAttention cut wasted KV-cache memory to under 4% (2023).`]},"tech:mlapi":{intro:`Machine learning services sell artificial intelligence as a utility over the internet. A developer who wants to recognise faces in photos, transcribe phone calls, translate web pages or detect the language of a message sends the data to a cloud service and gets the answer back, paying per request, with no need to hire researchers or train models.`,how:`The provider trains large models on its own vast datasets and runs them in its data centres behind a simple web API: send an image, receive labels such as “dog” or “beach” with confidence scores. Prices are typically a fraction of a cent per request. Customers can sometimes upload their own examples to customise a model. The provider's scale gives it more data and cheaper computing than any single customer could manage.`,history:`In December 2011 Google began charging for its Translate API, which had been free. In November 2013 IBM announced it would open Watson, its Jeopardy-winning question-answering system, to developers as a cloud service. In April 2015 Microsoft launched Project Oxford, with face, speech and vision APIs. In February 2016 Google opened its Cloud Vision API to the public in beta. In November 2016 Amazon announced Rekognition for images, Polly for speech and Lex for chatbots. In January 2018 Google's AutoML Vision let customers train custom image models without writing code.`,facts:[`Microsoft's how-old.net, a Project Oxford demo that guessed ages from photos, went viral in 2015.`,`Tests by the ACLU found Rekognition falsely matched 28 members of Congress with police mugshots (2018).`]},"tech:apiplat":{intro:`A model API lets any programmer use a large language model over the internet, paying by the amount of text it reads and writes. Instead of a dozen specialised services, one general model is told in plain English what to do: summarise this, translate that, write advertising copy, answer customer emails. Developers build products on it that the model's makers never thought of.`,how:`Text is measured in tokens, pieces of words about three quarters of a word long on average, and priced per thousand or per million. A request sends a prompt, perhaps with instructions and examples, and receives the model's continuation. The provider runs the model on its own GPUs, batching many customers together, and screens applications to guard against spam and abuse. Building on someone else's model ties a product to that provider's prices and policies.`,history:`In June 2020 OpenAI opened a private beta of its API, serving GPT-3, and early users built writing assistants, code generators and text adventure games. In 2021 Cohere in Toronto launched a rival language model API. In November 2021 OpenAI dropped its waiting list and opened the API to developers in most countries. In March 2023 OpenAI released the model behind ChatGPT, gpt-3.5-turbo, at a tenth of the price of its previous best. In March 2023 Anthropic opened its Claude API and Google announced its PaLM API.`,facts:[`GPT-3's biggest model, Davinci, cost 6 cents per thousand tokens (2020).`,`gpt-3.5-turbo cost 0.2 cents per thousand tokens (2023).`]},"tech:chatui":{intro:`A chat assistant puts a language model behind a simple conversation window that anyone can use. You type a question or a request in ordinary words, and it replies in fluent paragraphs, remembers what was said earlier in the chat, and can be asked to try again, explain more simply or change its tone. No programming and no specialist prompts: just talk.`,how:`The model is pre-trained on huge amounts of text, then fine-tuned on example dialogues and with human feedback to be helpful and to decline harmful requests. The website sends the whole conversation so far with each new message, so the model sees the context. Replies stream onto the screen word by word as they are generated. Every answer costs real computing, so a popular assistant needs an enormous fleet of GPUs.`,history:`In 1966 Joseph Weizenbaum's ELIZA at MIT imitated a therapist with simple pattern matching, and some users confided in it anyway. In March 2016 Microsoft's Tay chatbot was taught by Twitter users to post offensive messages and was taken down within a day. On 30 November 2022 OpenAI released ChatGPT as a free “research preview”. By January 2023 it had an estimated 100 million monthly users, then the fastest growth of any consumer app. In February 2023 Microsoft added a chat assistant to Bing, and in March 2023 Anthropic released Claude and Google opened Bard. In October 2025 OpenAI said ChatGPT had 800 million weekly users.`,facts:[`ChatGPT reached a million users five days after launch (2022).`,`ChatGPT had about 100 million monthly users two months after launch (2023).`,`ChatGPT had 800 million weekly users (2025).`]},"tech:copilot":{intro:`A code assistant is autocomplete for programmers, driven by a language model trained on billions of lines of public code. As a developer types, it suggests the rest of the line or a whole function, often from nothing more than a descriptive name or a comment. It takes over much of the boilerplate of programming, though its suggestions can hide subtle bugs and need checking.`,how:`The model reads the code around the cursor, the open file and sometimes other files in the project, and predicts what comes next, just as a text model predicts the next word. The editor shows the suggestion in grey, and a press of the Tab key accepts it. Speed matters more than brilliance: suggestions must appear within a fraction of a second, so the serving model is smaller than the best chat models.`,history:`In July 2019 the start-up TabNine used GPT-2 to autocomplete code in many programming languages. In June 2021 GitHub, owned by Microsoft, previewed Copilot, built on OpenAI's Codex, a GPT-3 model fine-tuned on public code from GitHub. In July 2021 OpenAI's Codex paper reported that it solved 28.8% of a set of Python problems on the first try, against none for GPT-3. In June 2022 Copilot became a paid product at 10 dollars a month. In November 2022 programmers filed a class-action lawsuit claiming Copilot reproduced licensed code without credit.`,facts:[`In a GitHub study, Copilot users finished a coding task 55% faster (2022).`,`Copilot had about 1.3 million paying subscribers (2024).`]},"tech:codeagent":{intro:`A coding agent is an AI that does programming tasks from start to finish rather than suggesting the next line. Given a repository and a request, such as fixing a bug, adding a feature or upgrading a library, it reads the code, makes a plan, edits files across the project, runs the tests, reads the errors and keeps going until the work is done, then hands it back for review.`,how:`The agent runs a language model in a loop with tools: a terminal for running commands and tests, a file editor, and search over the codebase. It works either on the developer's own machine or in a cloud sandbox, where it can run unsupervised and open a pull request. What matters most is the model's long-horizon reasoning and its habit of checking its own changes; benchmarks score it on real bugs from open-source projects.`,history:`In October 2023 Princeton researchers released SWE-bench, 2,294 real bugs taken from GitHub issues in Python projects. In March 2024 the start-up Cognition demonstrated Devin, billed as an “AI software engineer”. In February 2025 Anthropic previewed Claude Code, a coding agent that works in the terminal, and in May 2025 OpenAI launched its cloud agent Codex. In June 2025 the AI code editor Cursor was reported to earn 500 million dollars a year. In May 2025 Claude Opus 4 solved 72.5% of SWE-bench Verified, a checked set of 500 of the problems. In November 2025 Claude Opus 4.5 passed 80%.`,facts:[`The best model of 2023 solved under 2% of SWE-bench's bugs.`,`Claude Opus 4.5 solved 80.9% of SWE-bench Verified (2025).`]},"tech:imageapp":{intro:`An image studio is a consumer app that turns a written description into pictures within seconds: a lighthouse in a storm, painted like Turner. Users can ask for variations, change the style, enlarge an image or edit part of it. It puts an illustrator's skills in anyone's hands, and it set off fierce arguments among artists about training on their work without permission.`,how:`Behind the app sits a text-to-image model, usually a diffusion model, running on banks of cloud GPUs. The user's prompt is encoded, the model generates several candidates at once, and the user picks one to upscale or vary. Subscriptions pay for the GPU time. Filters block certain words and check the output to stop violent or explicit images, and pictures of real people in compromising scenes.`,history:`In January 2021 OpenAI announced DALL-E, which could draw “an armchair in the shape of an avocado”. In April 2022 DALL-E 2 produced photorealistic images and opened to a waiting list. In July 2022 Midjourney, a small lab led by David Holz, opened a public beta on the chat platform Discord, where users typed prompts in shared channels. In August 2022 a Midjourney picture, Théâtre D'opéra Spatial by Jason Allen, won the digital art category at the Colorado State Fair. In January 2023 artists including Sarah Andersen sued Stability AI, Midjourney and DeviantArt over training on their work. In March 2025 OpenAI's GPT-4o image generation set off a craze for portraits in the style of Studio Ghibli.`,facts:[`Jason Allen's prize for his Midjourney picture was 300 dollars (2022).`,`ChatGPT users made over 700 million images in the first week of GPT-4o images (2025).`]},"tech:voice":{intro:`Voice mode lets you talk to an AI assistant out loud and hear it answer, as naturally as a phone call. The best versions respond in a fraction of a second, can be interrupted mid-sentence, catch tone of voice and reply with expression: laughing, whispering, sounding sympathetic. The assistant becomes something to chat with while cooking or driving, not just something to type to.`,how:`Older voice assistants chained three separate models: speech recognition turned your voice into text, a language model wrote a reply, and a speech synthesiser read it out, losing tone and adding seconds of delay. A natively multimodal model handles audio directly, taking in sound tokens and producing sound tokens in one network, so it hears emotion and pauses and can start speaking almost at once. Telling when the user has finished speaking is surprisingly hard.`,history:`In October 2011 Apple's Siri brought a voice assistant to the iPhone 4S, though it handled only fixed kinds of request. In September 2023 OpenAI added voice conversations to the ChatGPT app, using Whisper to listen and a new speech model to answer. In May 2024 OpenAI demonstrated GPT-4o's voice, which could laugh, sing and be interrupted. In May 2024 the actor Scarlett Johansson objected that one of its voices, Sky, sounded like hers, and OpenAI paused it. In July 2024 Advanced Voice Mode began rolling out to paying users. In October 2024 OpenAI's Realtime API let developers build speech-to-speech apps on the same model.`,facts:[`GPT-4o could answer speech in as little as 232 milliseconds (2024).`,`People in conversation leave gaps of about 200 milliseconds between turns, a ten-language study found (2009).`]},"tech:ondevice":{intro:`On-device AI runs a language model directly on a phone or laptop instead of in a distant data centre. Answers arrive instantly, work without a signal, cost nothing per question and never send your messages or photos to a server. The catch is size: a phone's memory and battery allow a model of only a few billion parameters, far smaller than the cloud's giants.`,how:`A model of about 3 billion parameters is distilled from a larger one and compressed to around 4 bits per weight, so it fits in a couple of gigabytes of memory. It runs on the phone chip's neural engine, which does the matrix maths efficiently on battery power. Small “adapters” specialise it for tasks such as summarising notifications, rewriting emails or tidying photos. Requests that are too hard can be passed to a bigger model in the cloud.`,history:`In 2017 Apple's A11 Bionic chip added a neural engine, used for Face ID and photo effects. In December 2023 Google's Pixel 8 Pro ran Gemini Nano, its smallest model, on the phone to summarise recordings and suggest replies. In January 2024 Samsung's Galaxy S24 launched Galaxy AI, with live call translation partly done on the phone. In June 2024 Apple announced Apple Intelligence, built around a model on the device and larger ones on its own Private Cloud Compute servers. In 2024 Apple Intelligence needed at least 8 GB of memory, ruling out every iPhone older than the iPhone 15 Pro. In June 2025 Apple opened its on-device model to app developers through its Foundation Models framework.`,facts:[`Apple's on-device model had about 3 billion parameters (2024).`,`Apple compressed it to under 4 bits per weight on average (2024).`]},"tech:cloudplat":{intro:`Cloud computing rents servers over the internet by the hour to anyone with a credit card. Instead of buying machines, waiting weeks for delivery and hiring staff to run them, a start-up can switch on a hundred virtual servers in minutes, use them for an afternoon and switch them off. Computing becomes a utility bill rather than a capital purchase.`,how:`The provider fills huge data centres with identical commodity servers and uses virtualisation software to carve each physical machine into several virtual ones, each walled off from its neighbours. Customers start and stop machines through a web page or an API and pay only for the hours used, while storage is rented by the gigabyte per month. The provider profits from scale, buying hardware and electricity cheaper than anyone else and keeping its machines busy across thousands of customers.`,history:`In March 2006 Amazon Web Services launched S3, online storage at 15 cents a gigabyte a month. In August 2006 it launched EC2 in beta, renting virtual servers by the hour. In April 2008 Google launched App Engine, and in February 2010 Microsoft's Windows Azure became commercially available. In November 2010 AWS began renting GPU machines with NVIDIA Tesla cards. In April 2015 Amazon published AWS's results for the first time, showing it was highly profitable. In 2017 three commodity traders in New Jersey set up a cryptocurrency-mining firm that, renamed CoreWeave, turned to renting GPUs in 2019.`,facts:[`EC2's first virtual server rented for 10 cents an hour (2006).`,`AWS earned about 7.9 billion dollars in 2015.`,`AWS revenue reached about 108 billion dollars in 2024.`]},"tech:selfdrive":{intro:`A self-driving stack is the software that lets a car drive itself, traditionally split into stages. Perception works out what is around the car from cameras, radar and laser scanners; prediction guesses what every pedestrian and driver will do next; planning picks a safe, comfortable path; and control turns that into steering, braking and throttle. Each piece is built and tested separately.`,how:`Lidar sweeps laser beams around the car to measure distances in every direction, building a 3D point cloud many times a second, while deep neural networks pick out cars, cyclists and traffic lights in camera images. The car locates itself to within centimetres on a detailed map of the streets made in advance. Prediction and planning mix learned models with hand-written rules. Rare situations, a mattress on the motorway or a police officer waving traffic through, are the hardest part.`,history:`In 2009 Google started a secret self-driving car project led by Sebastian Thrun, whose Stanford team had won the 2005 DARPA Grand Challenge. In October 2014 Tesla began fitting cars with hardware for Autopilot, a driver assistance system for motorways. In May 2016 a Tesla driver in Florida died when his car on Autopilot failed to see a lorry crossing the road. In December 2016 Google's project became a separate Alphabet company, Waymo. In March 2018 an Uber test car killed a pedestrian, Elaine Herzberg, in Tempe, Arizona. In December 2018 Waymo launched a paid robotaxi service in suburban Phoenix, and in October 2020 it opened rides with nobody at the wheel to the public there.`,facts:[`Waymo's cars had driven 20 million miles on public roads by January 2020.`,`Waymo was giving more than 250,000 paid rides a week in 2025.`]},"tech:e2edrive":{intro:`End-to-end driving replaces most of the hand-written code in a self-driving car with one large neural network that goes straight from camera images to steering and acceleration. Instead of engineers writing rules for every situation, the network learns to drive by watching millions of clips of good human driving. It copes with odd situations more smoothly but is harder to inspect when it goes wrong.`,how:`Video from eight or so cameras feeds a network that builds its own internal picture of the scene and outputs the car's planned path. Training data comes from a huge fleet: the company selects clips where drivers handled tricky situations well and trains the network to imitate them. Simulation and generated video supply rare scenarios. With no explicit rules to audit, testing relies on vast numbers of driven and simulated miles.`,history:`In 1989 Dean Pomerleau's ALVINN at Carnegie Mellon steered a van using a small neural network fed by a camera. In April 2016 NVIDIA researchers trained a single convolutional network, DAVE-2, to steer a car on public roads from camera images alone. In July 2018 the London start-up Wayve showed a car learning to follow a country lane by trial and error in a single day. In March 2024 Tesla rolled out version 12 of its Full Self-Driving software, which replaced most of its hand-written driving code with neural networks. In June 2025 Tesla began a small robotaxi service in Austin, Texas, at first with a safety monitor in the passenger seat.`,facts:[`NVIDIA's DAVE-2 learned to steer from about 72 hours of human driving (2016).`,`Tesla said version 12 replaced over 300,000 lines of hand-written code (2024).`]},"tech:qcloud":{intro:`A quantum cloud puts a real quantum computer on the internet so that scientists, students and companies can run programs on it without owning one. The machines are delicate and expensive, sealed in refrigerators colder than outer space, so sharing them makes sense. Users design circuits in a web page or in code, join a queue and get back the measured results.`,how:`A user writes a quantum circuit, a sequence of operations on qubits, with a drag-and-drop composer or a programming toolkit, and submits it as a job. The provider translates it for the particular chip's layout and native operations and runs it thousands of times, because each run gives a random outcome, then returns the counts of each result. Simulators on ordinary computers let people test small circuits first.`,history:`In May 2016 IBM put a 5-qubit superconducting processor online as the IBM Quantum Experience, free for anyone to program from a web browser. In March 2017 IBM released Qiskit, an open-source Python toolkit for writing quantum programs. In June 2017 the California start-up Rigetti Computing opened its own quantum cloud, Forest. In November 2019 Microsoft announced Azure Quantum, and in December 2019 Amazon announced Braket, both selling time on machines from companies such as IonQ and Rigetti. In November 2021 IBM unveiled its 127-qubit Eagle processor for its cloud users.`,facts:[`IBM's first cloud quantum processor had 5 qubits (2016).`,`IBM's Eagle processor had 127 qubits (2021).`]},"tech:asic":{intro:`A custom AI chip, an application-specific integrated circuit or ASIC, is designed to do one job: the matrix multiplications at the heart of neural networks. It drops the graphics features, large caches and general-purpose logic of a CPU or GPU and fills the silicon with multipliers and memory instead. For that one job it is many times faster per watt and per dollar than general chips.`,how:`Neural networks spend nearly all their time multiplying large grids of numbers and adding up the results, so the chip devotes most of its area to a huge array of small multiply-accumulate units fed from on-chip memory. It works at low precision, such as 8-bit integers for inference, since networks tolerate rounding. A host computer feeds it instructions over a standard expansion slot. A design costs tens of millions of dollars, which pays off only at huge scale.`,history:`In 2013 Google engineers calculated that if every Android user spoke to voice search for three minutes a day, Google would need to double its data centres. In 2015 Google began running its first Tensor Processing Unit, designed, built and deployed in about 15 months. In May 2016 Sundar Pichai revealed the TPU at Google I/O and said it had helped AlphaGo beat Lee Sedol. In August 2016 Intel bought the AI chip start-up Nervana for a reported 400 million dollars. In April 2017 Norman Jouppi and colleagues reported that the TPU ran inference 15 to 30 times faster than contemporary CPUs and GPUs, with 30 to 80 times better performance per watt. In December 2019 Intel bought another AI chip maker for about 2 billion dollars.`,facts:[`TPU v1 had 65,536 8-bit multipliers in a 256 by 256 grid (2015).`,`It peaked at 92 trillion operations a second, drawing about 40 watts when busy (2015).`]},"tech:systolic":{intro:`A tensor core is a block of a chip that multiplies small matrices in one step, rather than doing one multiplication at a time. Built as a grid of multipliers through which numbers flow in a steady rhythm, it delivers many times more arithmetic per clock tick and per watt than ordinary processor cores. It is the engine that makes deep learning hardware fast.`,how:`In a systolic array, each cell multiplies the two numbers passing through it, adds the result to a running total and hands the numbers on to its neighbours, like a heart pumping blood in beats. Because data is reused as it flows, few trips to memory are needed. An NVIDIA tensor core multiplies two 4×4 matrices of 16-bit numbers and adds a third, 64 multiply-adds every clock, keeping results at 32-bit accuracy.`,history:`In 1978 H T Kung and Charles Leiserson at Carnegie Mellon described systolic arrays for chips. In 2015 Google's first TPU used a 256 by 256 systolic array of 8-bit multipliers. In May 2017 NVIDIA announced the Tesla V100, based on its Volta design, with 640 tensor cores. In September 2018 NVIDIA's Turing gaming cards added tensor cores, used to sharpen game graphics with an AI upscaler called DLSS. In May 2020 the A100's third-generation tensor cores added the TF32 and bfloat16 formats and a speed-up for matrices that are half zeros.`,facts:[`The V100 had 640 tensor cores and 21 billion transistors (2017).`,`A V100 did about 125 teraflops of tensor maths, six times a P100's 16-bit rate (2017).`]},"tech:tensor2":{intro:`Asynchronous tensor engines keep a chip's huge matrix units busy all the time. As matrix maths grew faster, the bottleneck became feeding it: processors wasted time fetching each tile of data before computing. A dedicated copy engine that moves the next block of data into fast memory while the current block is being multiplied lets data movement and computation overlap.`,how:`In NVIDIA's Hopper, the Tensor Memory Accelerator takes one instruction describing a whole multi-dimensional tile and copies it from the GPU's main memory into shared memory by itself, freeing the threads that would otherwise calculate addresses. Groups of threads issue large matrix operations that run asynchronously, while “thread block clusters” let neighbouring cores read each other's memory. Programmers build a pipeline: load tile three, compute tile two, write tile one.`,history:`In May 2020 NVIDIA's A100 introduced asynchronous copies from main memory to shared memory, a first step. In March 2022 NVIDIA announced the H100, Hopper, with fourth-generation tensor cores, the Tensor Memory Accelerator and thread block clusters, made on TSMC's 4N process. In 2022 NVIDIA quoted about three times the A100's 16-bit tensor speed for the H100. In 2023 NVIDIA's CUTLASS 3 library and in July 2024 FlashAttention-3 showed how to program the new engines, showing how to keep the new engines fed. In March 2024 NVIDIA's Blackwell added fifth-generation tensor cores with their own dedicated tensor memory.`,facts:[`The H100 had 80 billion transistors on a die of about 814 square millimetres (2022).`,`The H100 did about 989 teraflops of dense 16-bit tensor maths (2022).`]},"tech:fp8hw":{intro:`FP8 tensor cores are matrix units built to multiply 8-bit floating-point numbers. Halving the size of each number roughly halves the size of each multiplier, so twice as many fit on the same silicon, and twice as much data passes through the same wires. For work that tolerates less precision, mainly AI training and inference, that doubles the speed for little loss of accuracy.`,how:`Each FP8 multiplier handles a sign, a small exponent and a two- or three-bit mantissa, and the products are added up in a wider format so rounding errors do not pile up. Chips support two variants: E4M3, with more precision, for weights and activations, and E5M2, with more range, for gradients during training. Software tracks the largest value in each tensor and picks a scale factor so numbers neither overflow nor vanish.`,history:`In December 2018 IBM researchers showed that deep networks could be trained with 8-bit floating-point numbers using careful rounding. In March 2022 NVIDIA announced the H100, the first widely sold GPU with FP8 tensor cores, along with a Transformer Engine that chose precision layer by layer. In May 2022 Intel's Gaudi2 accelerator also supported FP8. In September 2022 NVIDIA, Arm and Intel jointly proposed the FP8 formats as an industry standard. In December 2023 AMD launched the MI300X with FP8 support. In December 2024 DeepSeek trained its V3 model mostly in FP8 on H800 GPUs, showing it worked at very large scale.`,facts:[`The largest E4M3 number is 448; the largest E5M2 number is 57,344 (2022).`,`DeepSeek-V3 trained in about 2.8 million H800 GPU hours, largely in FP8 (2024).`]},"tech:fp4hw":{intro:`FP4 tensor cores multiply numbers just four bits long, half the size of FP8. A four-bit number can take only 16 values, too crude on its own for anything useful; grouped into small blocks that share a common scale factor, it becomes good enough to run large language models. Each halving of size doubles the arithmetic a chip can do and halves the memory each number needs.`,how:`The E2M1 format packs a sign, two exponent bits and one mantissa bit. Blocks of 16 or 32 numbers share an extra scale factor, stored as an 8-bit value, that shifts the whole block to the right size, so large and small regions of a tensor each keep their detail. The tensor core reads a block, multiplies the tiny values, applies the scales and adds the products at higher precision.`,history:`In September 2023 the Open Compute Project published the microscaling formats, including MXFP4 with blocks of 32 values, agreed by AMD, Arm, Intel, Meta, Microsoft, NVIDIA and Qualcomm. In March 2024 NVIDIA announced Blackwell, the B200, with FP4 tensor cores and its own NVFP4 format with blocks of 16. In late 2024 the first B200 and GB200 systems shipped to customers. In June 2025 AMD launched its MI350 series with FP4 and FP6 support. In 2025 NVIDIA's Blackwell Ultra, the B300, raised FP4 throughput by half again. In September 2025 NVIDIA reported training a 12-billion-parameter model on 10 trillion tokens with NVFP4.`,facts:[`A B200 had 208 billion transistors on two dies (2024).`,`MXFP4 shares one scale between every 32 values (2023).`]},"tech:hbmint":{intro:`HBM integration places stacks of high-bandwidth memory right beside a processor in the same package, instead of on the circuit board around it. Memory and processor sit side by side on a slice of silicon called an interposer, which carries thousands of microscopic wires between them. The wide, short connections deliver far more data per second for far less power than ordinary graphics memory.`,how:`Graphics memory chips on a board each talk to the processor over a 32-bit channel, driven at very high speed down long copper traces. An HBM stack instead offers 1,024 data wires, running slower, over a few millimetres. A silicon interposer, made like a chip but carrying only wiring, can route such dense connections where a normal package cannot. Memory controller and interposer must be designed together, and the assembly is fragile and costly to package.`,history:`In 2008 AMD began working with the Korean memory maker SK hynix on stacked memory for graphics. In October 2013 the standards body JEDEC published the HBM standard. In June 2015 AMD launched the Radeon R9 Fury X, whose Fiji GPU sat on an interposer beside four HBM stacks. In 2016 NVIDIA's Tesla P100 used HBM2 on TSMC's CoWoS packaging, reaching 720 GB a second. In February 2019 AMD's Radeon VII was among the last gaming cards with HBM; from July 2019 AMD's gaming cards returned to cheaper GDDR6, leaving HBM to data-centre chips.`,facts:[`The Fury X's four HBM stacks gave a 4,096-bit memory bus (2015).`,`The Fury X moved 512 GB a second from just 4 GB of memory (2015).`]},"tech:chiplets":{intro:`Chiplets build a processor from several smaller dies joined in one package instead of a single large piece of silicon. Small dies have fewer defects each, so more of them work; different parts can be made on different processes, cutting-edge for the cores and cheaper for input and output; and a design can exceed the largest area a lithography machine can print in one exposure.`,how:`A lithography scanner can print an area of at most about 26 by 33 millimetres, the “reticle limit”, so no single chip can be larger. Chiplets sit side by side on an organic substrate or a silicon interposer, or are stacked, and talk through very short, wide links moving terabytes a second. Making the dies look like one chip to software depends on those links being fast and frugal, so packaging becomes as important as transistors.`,history:`In June 2017 AMD's first Epyc server processor combined four identical dies in one package. In July 2019 AMD's Zen 2 Ryzen and in August 2019 Epyc Rome put eight-core chiplets made on TSMC's 7 nm around a central input-output die made at 12 or 14 nm by GlobalFoundries. In March 2022 Intel, AMD, Arm, TSMC, Samsung and others founded the UCIe consortium to standardise links between chiplets. In December 2023 AMD's MI300X accelerator stacked eight compute dies on four input-output dies, with eight HBM stacks around them. In March 2024 NVIDIA's B200 joined two reticle-sized dies with a 10 TB a second link so they behaved as one GPU.`,facts:[`AMD's MI300X packed 153 billion transistors across 12 chiplets (2023).`,`The reticle limit is about 858 square millimetres (2019).`]},"tech:nvlink":{intro:`A scale-up interconnect is a private, very fast link that joins the processors inside one server, or one rack, so they can share data almost as if they were a single chip. Training a large neural network across several GPUs means constantly swapping partial results; over the computer's ordinary expansion bus or the network, the GPUs would spend most of their time waiting.`,how:`Instead of using the PCI Express bus, each GPU gets several dedicated high-speed serial links running directly to other GPUs, or to a switch chip that connects many GPUs at full speed. GPUs can read and write each other's memory directly, so collective operations such as averaging gradients across all of them run far faster. Links are short copper traces or cables, which keep them cheap and low in power but limit how far they reach.`,history:`In March 2014 NVIDIA announced NVLink, and in April 2016 it shipped on the Tesla P100, with four links. In April 2016 NVIDIA's DGX-1 server joined eight P100s in a “hybrid cube mesh”. In March 2018 NVIDIA's NVSwitch chip in the DGX-2 let 16 V100s all talk to each other at full speed. In 2022 the H100 had 18 NVLink links. In May 2024 AMD, Broadcom, Cisco, Google, HPE, Intel, Meta and Microsoft formed the UALink consortium to build an open rival. In May 2025 NVIDIA announced NVLink Fusion, letting other companies' chips join NVLink networks.`,facts:[`P100's NVLink moved 160 GB a second, five times PCI Express 3.0 (2016).`,`An H100 had 900 GB a second of NVLink bandwidth (2022).`]},"tech:rackscale":{intro:`A rack-scale system treats an entire rack of computers, dozens of accelerators with their power, cooling and switches, as one product and one giant computer. Wiring all the chips together with fast links inside the rack lets a model too large for any single server be split across them as if they shared one memory. The rack, not the chip, becomes the unit customers buy.`,how:`In NVIDIA's GB200 NVL72, 18 compute trays each hold four Blackwell GPUs and two Grace CPUs, and nine switch trays connect all 72 GPUs at full speed. Instead of optical cables, a “spine” of copper cables runs down the back of the rack, which uses less power and fails less often over such short distances. The rack draws around 120 kilowatts, far more than air can remove, so the chips are cooled by liquid.`,history:`In March 2018 NVIDIA's DGX-2 joined 16 GPUs into one NVLink network inside a single server. In May 2023 NVIDIA announced the DGX GH200, linking 256 Grace Hopper chips over NVLink into one system with 144 TB of shared memory. In March 2024 NVIDIA unveiled the GB200 NVL72, a single rack of 72 GPUs acting as one, with 130 TB a second of NVLink bandwidth inside it. In late 2024 the first NVL72 racks reached customers, and in 2025 shipments ramped up after early reports of overheating and connection faults. In April 2025 Google announced Ironwood TPU pods of 9,216 chips joined by its own interconnect. In June 2025 AMD announced Helios, its own 72-GPU rack.`,facts:[`An NVL72 rack's copper spine held over 5,000 cables, about two miles of wire (2024).`,`One NVL72 rack drew about 120 kilowatts (2024).`]},"tech:optics":{intro:`Co-packaged optics moves the lasers and light detectors that carry data over fibre from plug-in modules at the front of a network switch to the edge of the switch chip itself. Electrical signals then travel millimetres instead of tens of centimetres before being turned into light. That cuts the power needed to move each bit, a growing share of an AI data centre's energy bill.`,how:`In a normal switch, the chip sends electrical signals across the circuit board to pluggable transceivers, each with its own signal processor to clean up the signal and drive a laser. With co-packaged optics, silicon photonic engines, chips that guide light in tiny waveguides and modulate it with electrical signals, sit in the same package as the switch chip. The lasers themselves are kept in separate replaceable modules, since they are the part most likely to fail.`,history:`In August 2016 Intel began selling silicon photonics transceivers for data centres. In March 2020 Intel demonstrated optics co-packaged with a Barefoot Tofino switch chip. In 2024 Broadcom delivered Bailly, an Ethernet switch with co-packaged optics. In March 2025 NVIDIA announced its Quantum-X and Spectrum-X Photonics switches, using co-packaged optics made with TSMC. In 2025 NVIDIA said its InfiniBand photonic switches would ship first, with Ethernet versions following about a year after.`,facts:[`NVIDIA claimed its co-packaged optics used 3.5 times less power than pluggable modules (2025).`,`Broadcom's Bailly switched 51.2 terabits a second (2024).`]},"tech:sram":{intro:`An SRAM inference chip keeps a neural network's weights in the fast static memory built into the chip itself, rather than in separate DRAM or HBM. On-chip SRAM can be read many times faster than any external memory, so words come out at very high speed. The catch is capacity: each chip holds only a few hundred megabytes, so a large model must be spread across hundreds of chips.`,how:`Groq's design is deterministic: there are no caches or unpredictable scheduling, and its compiler plans every movement and calculation in advance, down to the clock cycle. Each chip holds a slice of the model's layers in SRAM and passes results to the next chip over direct links, like an assembly line. With nothing waiting on memory, a single user's request runs fast. The cost is many chips per model, so it suits fast serving more than training.`,history:`In 2016 Jonathan Ross, who had helped start Google's TPU project, founded Groq in Mountain View, California. In 2018 Britain's Graphcore began shipping its IPU, which also held models in on-chip SRAM, about 300 MB of it. In 2019 Groq unveiled its first chip, the Tensor Streaming Processor, made on a 14 nm process. In February 2024 a public Groq demo served Meta's Llama 2 70B at around 300 tokens a second per user, many times faster than GPU services. In December 2025 NVIDIA paid about 20 billion dollars to license Groq's technology and hire its leaders, including Ross.`,facts:[`Groq's first chip held 230 MB of SRAM with 80 TB a second of internal bandwidth (2019).`,`Serving a 70-billion-parameter model took several hundred Groq chips (2024).`]},"tech:wafer":{intro:`A wafer-scale engine is a single chip made from almost an entire silicon wafer, instead of cutting the wafer into dozens of separate chips. Everything that would otherwise be joined by slower cables and circuit boards, hundreds of thousands of cores and their memory, sits on one continuous piece of silicon, wired together at on-chip speed. It is the biggest chip ever made.`,how:`Every wafer has defects, which normally ruin whole chips. A wafer-scale design is a grid of tiny identical cores with spare cores and spare links; after testing, faulty ones are switched off and traffic is routed around them. Wires are drawn across the gaps between the scanner's exposure fields to join them up. Its memory is all on-chip SRAM, and it needs a custom package that feeds it many kilowatts and cools it with liquid.`,history:`In 1980 Gene Amdahl founded Trilogy Systems to build wafer-scale computers; it abandoned the attempt in 1984, beaten by defects. In August 2019 Cerebras Systems, a California start-up led by Andrew Feldman, unveiled its Wafer Scale Engine, with 400,000 cores. In April 2021 the WSE-2 more than doubled that to 2.6 trillion transistors on TSMC's 7 nm. In March 2024 the WSE-3 reached 4 trillion transistors and 900,000 cores. In August 2024 Cerebras launched an inference service that ran Llama 3.1 8B at about 1,800 tokens a second.`,facts:[`The first WSE had 1.2 trillion transistors and 18 GB of SRAM (2019).`,`The WSE-3 measured 46,225 square millimetres, 57 times an H100 (2024).`,`A Cerebras CS-1 system drew about 20 kilowatts (2019).`]},"tech:mobilesoc":{intro:`A mobile system-on-chip puts nearly a whole computer onto one small piece of silicon for a phone or tablet: the main processor cores, a graphics processor, image and video processors, memory controllers and often the cellular modem. Everything is designed to sip power so the battery lasts a day, and to stay cool without a fan in a case a few millimetres thick.`,how:`Different jobs go to specialised blocks: the video decoder plays a film on a fraction of the power the CPU would need, and the image signal processor cleans up photos from the camera. Arm's “big.LITTLE” approach pairs fast, power-hungry cores with small, frugal ones and switches between them as the load changes. A 64-bit instruction set gives more registers and room for more memory, and parts of the chip shut down entirely when idle.`,history:`In 2007 the first iPhone used a Samsung-made chip with an Arm11 processor core. In 2008 the first phones with Qualcomm's Snapdragon, which combined processor and modem in one chip, went on sale. In 2010 Apple's first in-house design, the A4, appeared in the iPad and the iPhone 4. In October 2011 Arm announced its 64-bit ARMv8 architecture. In September 2013 Apple's A7 in the iPhone 5s became the first 64-bit chip in a phone, and in 2013 Samsung's Galaxy S4 used Arm's big.LITTLE design. In November 2020 Apple's M1, a scaled-up phone chip, replaced Intel processors in Macs.`,facts:[`The A7 had over a billion transistors on 102 square millimetres (2013).`,`The M1 had 16 billion transistors (2020).`]},"tech:npu":{intro:`A neural engine, or neural processing unit, is a dedicated block inside a phone or laptop chip for running neural networks. It does the matrix multiplications of AI models far more efficiently than the main processor or graphics unit, so features such as face recognition, photo enhancement and voice transcription can run constantly on the device without draining the battery or sending data to the cloud.`,how:`Like a data-centre AI chip in miniature, a neural engine is mostly an array of multiply-accumulate units working on low-precision numbers, 8-bit or 16-bit, with local memory to avoid costly trips to the phone's main memory. Its speed is quoted in TOPS, trillions of operations a second. Software converts trained models into a form the engine can run, and falls back to the CPU or GPU for anything it cannot handle.`,history:`In September 2017 Huawei's Kirin 970 and Apple's A11 Bionic both added neural processors, Apple's two-core neural engine handling Face ID and Animoji. In 2018 Apple's A12 neural engine grew to eight cores. In November 2020 Apple's M1 brought a 16-core neural engine to the Mac. In December 2023 Intel's Core Ultra processors gave Windows laptops an NPU. In May 2024 Microsoft required at least 40 TOPS of NPU speed for its Copilot+ PC label, and Apple's M4 reached 38 TOPS.`,facts:[`Apple's first neural engine did 600 billion operations a second (2017).`,`Apple's A12 neural engine did 5 trillion operations a second (2018).`,`The M4's neural engine was about 60 times faster than the A11's (2024).`]},"tech:photonic":{intro:`Photonic computing would do a neural network's matrix multiplications with light instead of electrons. Beams of laser light, their brightness or phase encoding numbers, pass through a mesh of tiny waveguides and interferometers on a chip; the way the waves combine performs the multiplication almost instantly and with very little energy. Electronic chips would still handle memory and the parts that need precision.`,how:`A matrix can be broken down into a network of beam splitters and phase shifters, so light entering one side emerges with its strengths multiplied by the matrix. Changing the phase settings reprograms the weights. The difficulty is everything around that core: converting digital numbers into light and back costs energy, analogue light signals are noisy, limiting precision to a few bits, and optical parts are large compared with transistors.`,history:`In 1994 Michael Reck and colleagues showed that any such matrix operation on light could be built from a mesh of beam splitters and phase shifters. In June 2017 Yichen Shen, Nicholas Harris and colleagues at MIT ran a simple speech recognition network on a programmable photonic chip, reported in Nature Photonics. In 2017 Harris co-founded Lightmatter in Boston and Shen founded Lightelligence. In October 2024 Lightmatter raised 400 million dollars, mostly for Passage, its optical links between chips. In April 2025 Lightmatter reported in Nature a photonic processor that ran networks such as ResNet and BERT with accuracy close to electronic chips. Forecasters expected practical photonic AI processors around 2032.`,facts:[`MIT's photonic chip recognised spoken vowels with about 77% accuracy (2017).`,`Lightmatter was valued at 4.4 billion dollars (2024).`]},"tech:neuro":{intro:`A neuromorphic chip computes in a way loosely modelled on the brain. Instead of a central processor fetching instructions and data from a separate memory, it has many small cores, each simulating a group of neurons with its own memory beside it, which communicate by sending brief “spikes” only when something changes. The result can be extraordinarily frugal with power.`,how:`Each core stores the connection strengths, the synapses, for its neurons and adds up the spikes arriving from other cores; when a neuron's total crosses a threshold it fires a spike of its own, sent across the chip as a tiny packet. Because nothing happens without a spike, idle parts use almost no power. Programming is the difficulty: standard deep learning tools cannot train spiking networks directly.`,history:`In the late 1980s Carver Mead at Caltech coined the word “neuromorphic” for analogue chips that mimic nerve cells. In 2008 the US defence research agency DARPA's SyNAPSE programme began funding IBM and others to build brain-like chips. In August 2014 IBM unveiled TrueNorth in the journal Science, with 5.4 billion transistors, a million neurons and 256 million synapses. In September 2017 Intel announced Loihi, which could learn on the chip. In October 2023 IBM described NorthPole, which put memory beside compute for ordinary neural networks. In April 2024 Intel's Hala Point system combined 1,152 Loihi 2 chips.`,facts:[`TrueNorth ran a million artificial neurons on about 70 milliwatts (2014).`,`Hala Point simulated 1.15 billion neurons (2024).`,`NorthPole recognised images about 25 times more efficiently than comparable GPUs (2023).`]},"tech:p28":{intro:`The 28 nm process is a generation of chipmaking in which the smallest features of a transistor are about 28 billionths of a metre across. It combines flat, “planar” transistors with gates of metal over an insulator of hafnium-based material, printed with deep-ultraviolet light shone through water. It packs roughly twice as many transistors into an area as the 40 nm node before it.`,how:`A 193 nanometre argon fluoride laser shines through a mask and a lens system onto the wafer, with a thin layer of ultrapure water between lens and wafer bending the light more sharply so finer lines can be printed: immersion lithography. High-k metal gates replace the old silicon dioxide insulator, which had grown so thin that current leaked straight through it. Their thicker, better insulator holds the leak back while still letting the gate control the channel.`,history:`In 2007 Intel introduced high-k metal gates at its 45 nm node. In late 2011 TSMC began volume production at 28 nm, its first node with high-k metal gates, and in December 2011 AMD announced the Radeon HD 7970, the first 28 nm graphics chip. In 2012 shortages of 28 nm capacity held back Qualcomm's Snapdragon S4 phone chips and NVIDIA's Kepler GPUs. In 2013 the processors of Sony's PlayStation 4 and Microsoft's Xbox One were made at 28 nm. In 2021 shortages of chips made on older nodes such as 28 nm halted car production lines around the world.`,facts:[`The Radeon HD 7970's chip had 4.3 billion transistors (2011).`,`Intel's 45 nm chips were the first with high-k metal gates (2007).`]},"tech:p20":{intro:`The 20 nm process was the last generation of chipmaking to use flat, planar transistors. Shrinking them further made them leak current even when switched off, like a tap that cannot quite close, so 20 nm chips gained density but little speed or power saving. It was also the first node to need double patterning, two exposures per layer, on many layers, which raised costs.`,how:`In a planar transistor the gate sits on top of a flat channel and controls it from one side only. As the channel shortens, source and drain come so close that current sneaks through beneath the gate's control. At 20 nm leakage and variation between transistors grew severe. Printing lines this close with 193 nm light meant splitting each dense layer into two masks and exposing them separately, aligned to within a few nanometres.`,history:`In 2014 TSMC began volume production of its 20SoC process, and in September 2014 Apple's A8 for the iPhone 6 was one of its first big products. In 2011 Intel had already jumped to FinFET transistors at its 22 nm node, leaving planar designs behind. In 2015 Qualcomm's Snapdragon 810, made at 20 nm, gained a reputation for running hot in phones. In 2015 NVIDIA and AMD skipped 20 nm for their graphics chips, staying at 28 nm until FinFET processes arrived, because the node offered big chips too little benefit.`,facts:[`Apple's A8 had about 2 billion transistors on 89 square millimetres (2014).`,`The A8 was 13% smaller than the 28 nm A7 despite twice the transistors (2014).`]},"tech:p16":{intro:`The FinFET transistor stands its channel up as a thin vertical fin and wraps the gate over its top and both sides. With control from three sides instead of one, the gate can switch the current off firmly, stopping the leakage that crippled flat transistors. FinFETs gave chips a big jump in speed and power efficiency and let shrinking continue.`,how:`The fin is a slab of silicon only a few nanometres wide, etched up from the wafer surface, with the gate draped over it like a saddle. Several fins side by side make a transistor that needs to carry more current, so widths come in whole numbers of fins. Because the fin is so thin it needs little added impurity, so transistors vary less from one to the next. Making fins tall, thin and uniform was the manufacturing challenge.`,history:`In 1999 Chenming Hu, Tsu-Jae King Liu and Jeffrey Bokor at Berkeley described the FinFET, with funding from DARPA. In May 2011 Intel announced “tri-gate” FinFET transistors for its 22 nm node, shipping in Ivy Bridge processors in 2012. In 2015 Samsung's 14 nm and TSMC's 16 nm FinFET processes entered volume production, and Apple split its A9 chip between them. In 2016 NVIDIA's Pascal GPUs, including the P100 and the GTX 1080, were made on TSMC's 16 nm FinFET. In 2020 Chenming Hu received the IEEE Medal of Honor for the invention.`,facts:[`Apple's A9 was made by both Samsung at 14 nm and TSMC at 16 nm (2015).`,`NVIDIA's 16 nm GP100 had 15.3 billion transistors (2016).`]},"tech:p10":{intro:`The 10 nm generation shrank FinFET transistors further and, for the densest layers, pushed deep-ultraviolet lithography to extremes. With extreme-ultraviolet machines not yet ready, chipmakers printed the tightest wiring by splitting each pattern into several exposures. The node brought denser, more efficient phone chips but proved treacherous: the industry's long-time leader stumbled on it badly.`,how:`Self-aligned multiple patterning prints a set of lines, deposits thin spacer films on their sides, then etches away the original lines, leaving the spacers as a pattern at half the spacing; repeating the trick quarters it. Each extra step adds cost, time and chances for defects. Intel attempted an aggressive 10 nm with very dense wiring, cobalt in the finest wires and contacts placed directly over the transistor gates, which proved hard to make reliably.`,history:`In 2017 Samsung and TSMC entered volume production at 10 nm, with Samsung making Qualcomm's Snapdragon 835 and TSMC making Apple's A11 Bionic. In 2013 Intel had planned 10 nm for 2016. In May 2018 Intel shipped only a few 10 nm laptop chips, a Core i3 with its graphics switched off. In 2019 Intel's Ice Lake finally brought 10 nm to mainstream laptops. In June 2020 Apple announced its Macs would move from Intel processors to its own chips, made by TSMC. In July 2021 Intel renamed its improved 10 nm process “Intel 7”.`,facts:[`Intel's 10 nm aimed at 100.8 million transistors per square millimetre (2017).`,`Apple's 10 nm A11 Bionic had 4.3 billion transistors (2017).`]},"tech:p7":{intro:`The 7 nm process was the peak of deep-ultraviolet chipmaking: the finest patterns that 193 nm light, shone through water and split across multiple masks, could reasonably print. It roughly doubled transistor density again over 10 nm and powered a generation of phone chips, PC processors and AI accelerators. It also marked a change of leadership, as TSMC overtook Intel in manufacturing.`,how:`The tightest metal layers, with lines about 40 nanometres apart, were printed by self-aligned quadruple patterning, so a chip needed many dozens of masks. Each layer passes through repeated rounds of coating, exposure, deposition and etching, adding weeks to production. Designing a chip at 7 nm cost hundreds of millions of dollars, so only the biggest products used it.`,history:`In April 2018 TSMC began volume production at 7 nm, and in September 2018 Apple's A12 Bionic and Huawei's Kirin 980 were among the first 7 nm phone chips. In August 2018 GlobalFoundries abandoned its 7 nm programme, saying it could not afford to keep up, which sent AMD to TSMC. In July 2019 AMD's Zen 2 processors used TSMC 7 nm chiplets. In May 2020 NVIDIA's A100 was made on TSMC's 7 nm. In August 2023 China's SMIC made a 7 nm-class chip for Huawei's Mate 60 Pro without EUV machines, despite US export controls.`,facts:[`The A100 had 54 billion transistors on 826 square millimetres (2020).`,`Apple's A12 Bionic had 6.9 billion transistors (2018).`]},"tech:p5":{intro:`The 5 nm process was the first generation of chipmaking to rely heavily on extreme-ultraviolet light, with a wavelength of 13.5 nanometres instead of 193. The short wavelength prints in a single exposure the fine patterns that deep-ultraviolet tools needed three or four passes for, cutting steps and defects. It increased logic density by about 80% over 7 nm.`,how:`EUV light is made by blasting droplets of molten tin with a laser, and because air and glass absorb it, the whole beam path runs in vacuum and is focused by mirrors, not lenses. About fourteen of the chip's most critical layers are printed this way at TSMC's 5 nm. Fewer patterning steps mean simpler, more predictable manufacturing, though each EUV scanner costs over a hundred million dollars and processes wafers slowly.`,history:`In 2020 TSMC began volume production at 5 nm, with Apple's A14 in the iPhone 12 and then the M1 in Macs. In 2020 Samsung's 5 nm process also entered production, though its yields lagged behind TSMC's. In 2022 NVIDIA's H100 used a customised version of TSMC's 5 nm family called 4N. In late 2024 TSMC began making 4 nm chips at its new plant in Phoenix, Arizona.`,facts:[`Apple's A14, the first 5 nm chip, had 11.8 billion transistors (2020).`,`TSMC said its 5 nm packed about 1.8 times the logic of its 7 nm (2020).`]},"tech:p3":{intro:`The 3 nm process was TSMC's last generation of FinFET transistors, squeezing more fins and finer wires into each chip with heavier use of extreme-ultraviolet lithography. Logic circuits kept shrinking well, but the SRAM memory cells that make up a chip's caches barely got smaller, so the overall gain was modest and the cost of each wafer kept climbing.`,how:`TSMC's N3 family introduced “FinFlex”, letting designers mix cells with one, two or three fins to trade speed against power and area within the same chip. SRAM cells, six transistors each, hardly shrank, because their tightly packed contacts and wires had hit practical limits, so designs heavy with cache gained little. Each finished wafer reportedly cost around 20,000 dollars.`,history:`In June 2022 Samsung began producing 3 nm chips, its first with gate-all-around transistors rather than FinFETs. In December 2022 TSMC started volume production of N3 in Tainan. In December 2022 reports from the IEDM conference showed N3's SRAM cell was barely smaller than N5's. In September 2023 Apple's A17 Pro for the iPhone 15 Pro became the first 3 nm chip in a phone. In May 2024 the improved N3E went into Apple's M4.`,facts:[`TSMC's first N3 SRAM cell was only about 5% smaller than N5's (2022).`,`The A17 Pro had 19 billion transistors (2023).`]},"tech:p2":{intro:`The 2 nm generation replaces the FinFET with the gate-all-around transistor. Its channel is a stack of thin horizontal silicon ribbons, called nanosheets, with the gate material wrapped completely around each one. Surrounding the channel on all four sides, the gate controls the current even better, so transistors can shrink and run at lower voltage without leaking.`,how:`Manufacturers grow alternating layers of silicon and silicon-germanium, etch them into stacks, then dissolve the silicon-germanium to leave silicon sheets suspended like the shelves of a bookcase. The gate's insulator and metal are deposited into the gaps so they wrap every sheet. Sheets can be made wider for more current or narrower to save power, a freedom fins never had, since a fin's height was fixed for the whole chip.`,history:`In June 2017 IBM, with Samsung and GlobalFoundries, demonstrated stacked nanosheet transistors for a future 5 nm-class process. In May 2021 IBM showed a 2 nm test chip built with nanosheets. In June 2022 Samsung's 3 nm became the first gate-all-around process in production, with transistors Samsung called MBCFETs. In 2025 Intel's 18A process, with nanosheet transistors it called RibbonFETs, entered production for its Panther Lake laptop chips. In late 2025 TSMC began volume production of N2, its first nanosheet process, at fabs in Hsinchu and Kaohsiung.`,facts:[`IBM's 2 nm test chip fitted 50 billion transistors on a fingernail-sized area (2021).`,`TSMC said N2 used 25 to 30% less power than N3E at the same speed (2025).`]},"tech:pa16":{intro:`Backside power delivery moves the wires that supply electricity to a chip's transistors from above them to beneath them, on the back of the silicon. On ordinary chips, power and signal wiring share the same dense stack of metal layers on top. Putting the power underneath frees room for signals, cuts voltage losses and lets the transistors be packed closer together.`,how:`After the transistors and front wiring are built, the wafer is bonded face-down to a carrier wafer and ground away from the back until the transistors are almost exposed. Power wires and contacts are then built on this back side, reaching the transistors directly, like moving a house's plumbing into the basement. TSMC's “Super Power Rail” connects straight to each transistor's source and drain, a more direct but harder route than Intel's PowerVia, which connects through small vias beside the transistors.`,history:`In 2019 the Belgian research centre imec presented buried power rails fed from the back of the wafer as a way to keep shrinking chips. In June 2023 Intel showed test chips with PowerVia working on its Intel 4 process. In April 2024 TSMC announced A16, its 1.6 nm-class node with Super Power Rail, planned for production in the second half of 2026. In 2025 Intel's 18A became the first production process with backside power. Forecasters expected A16 chips in volume around 2027.`,facts:[`Intel's PowerVia test chip cut voltage droop by 30% and ran 6% faster (2023).`,`TSMC promised A16 would use 15 to 20% less power than N2P at the same speed (2024).`]},"tech:pa14":{intro:`A14 is TSMC's planned 1.4 nanometre-class process, a second generation of nanosheet transistors after the 2 nm family. Like every new node it aims to fit more transistors in the same area and run them faster or on less power, for the next generation of phone, PC and AI chips. Its name no longer measures any real feature: “1.4 nm” is a label for a generation.`,how:`Second-generation nanosheets refine the stacked silicon ribbons and their wrap-around gates, while TSMC's “NanoFlex Pro” lets designers mix short, power-saving cells and tall, fast ones in one chip. Wires and contacts shrink further, and a version with backside power delivery is planned. The tightest patterns are still printed with ordinary EUV machines rather than the newer high-NA ones, to keep costs down.`,history:`In April 2025 TSMC announced A14 at its technology symposium in Santa Clara, California, for production three years on. In April 2025 TSMC also said it did not need high-NA EUV machines for A14. In July 2025 Intel said its rival 14A process, which would use high-NA EUV, might be dropped if it did not win a major outside customer. Forecasters expected A14 chips around 2028.`,facts:[`TSMC promised A14 up to 30% less power than N2 at the same speed (2025).`,`TSMC claimed over 20% more logic density for A14 than N2 (2025).`]},"tech:pa10":{intro:`A forksheet transistor is a proposed step beyond the nanosheet. It places the n-type and p-type transistors, the two halves of every logic gate, side by side with a thin insulating wall between them, so their stacks of silicon sheets can sit much closer together. Removing the wide gap that normally separates them would shrink logic cells and SRAM by perhaps a fifth.`,how:`In a nanosheet design, n-type and p-type devices need generous space between them so their gates and contacts do not interfere. A forksheet builds a dielectric wall, a few nanometres thick, between the two stacks during manufacturing. The sheets attach to the wall on one side, so the gate wraps three sides of each sheet instead of four, like the tines of a fork. Printing such tight patterns would rely on high-NA EUV lithography.`,history:`In 2017 imec, the Belgian research centre, proposed the forksheet transistor, first for SRAM memory cells. In December 2019 imec presented simulations at the IEDM conference showing forksheets could shrink standard cells by about 20% compared with nanosheets. In June 2021 imec presented the first working forksheet test devices at the VLSI Symposium. In 2024 imec's roadmap placed forksheets after the 2 nm and 1.4 nm generations, at around the A10 node. Forecasters expected forksheet chips around 2030.`,facts:[`Imec's simulations showed about 20% smaller cells than nanosheets (2019).`,`A high-NA EUV scanner, needed for such patterns, cost about 380 million dollars (2024).`]},"tech:pa7":{intro:`A complementary FET, or CFET, would stack the n-type transistor directly on top of the p-type one instead of placing them side by side. Every basic logic gate needs one of each, so folding the pair vertically could nearly halve the area of a logic cell. It brings the third dimension to the transistor itself, after decades of shrinking chips in only two.`,how:`Two sets of nanosheets, one for each type, are built in a single tall stack, separated by an insulating layer, with gates wrapping both. In a “monolithic” CFET both are made in one sequence on the same wafer; in a “sequential” CFET the top layer is bonded on afterwards. Wiring up the bottom device without disturbing the top one is the great challenge, and backside power and contacts would be essential to reach both.`,history:`In 2018 imec proposed the CFET as a successor to nanosheet transistors. In December 2023 Intel, TSMC and Samsung each showed stacked CFET test devices at the IEDM conference in San Francisco. In 2023 Intel's version also fed power and contacts to the lower transistor from the back of the wafer. In 2024 imec's roadmap placed CFET after forksheets, at around the A7 node. Forecasters expected CFET chips in production around 2032.`,facts:[`TSMC's first stacked CFET test devices had a gate pitch of 48 nanometres (2023).`,`Three rival chipmakers showed CFET devices at a single conference (2023).`]},"tech:pa5":{intro:`Atomic-channel transistors would replace the silicon in a transistor's channel with a two-dimensional material just one to three atoms thick, such as molybdenum disulfide. When silicon sheets are thinned to a few nanometres, electrons move through them poorly; 2D materials keep conducting well even at atomic thickness, so the gate could control a far shorter channel.`,how:`A single layer of a transition-metal dichalcogenide, molybdenum or tungsten atoms sandwiched between sulfur or selenium, is grown across the wafer by chemical vapour deposition or transferred onto it. It would form the channels of stacked nanosheets or CFETs. The obstacles are growing flawless layers over a whole 300 mm wafer at temperatures that will not damage the rest of the chip, and making good electrical contacts to a sheet a few atoms thick.`,history:`In 2004 Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at the University of Manchester isolated graphene, the first two-dimensional material, and they won the Nobel prize in physics for it in 2010. In 2011 Andras Kis's group at EPFL in Lausanne built the first transistor from a single layer of molybdenum disulfide. In October 2016 a team at Berkeley Lab led by Ali Javey made a transistor with a gate just 1 nanometre long, using molybdenum disulfide and a carbon nanotube. In April 2025 researchers at Fudan University in Shanghai described in Nature a RISC-V processor built from molybdenum disulfide. Forecasters expected 2D channels in production around 2034, little more than a guess.`,facts:[`Kis's transistor used a molybdenum disulfide sheet about 0.65 nanometres thick (2011).`,`Fudan's 2D processor held about 5,900 transistors (2025).`]},"tech:euvuse":{intro:`EUV production means running extreme-ultraviolet lithography machines in a working chip factory, printing real products around the clock rather than test patterns in a laboratory. Each scanner, made only by the Dutch company ASML, is the size of a bus and costs over a hundred million euros. Learning to keep them running, feed them flawless masks and get enough power from their light sources took the industry decades.`,how:`Inside the machine a powerful carbon dioxide laser hits tiny droplets of molten tin tens of thousands of times a second, turning each into a plasma that glows at 13.5 nanometres. Because nearly everything absorbs this light, it is guided through vacuum by a series of ultra-smooth multilayer mirrors, each reflecting only about 70% of it. Masks are mirrors too, protected by a thin membrane called a pellicle; any speck of dust on them would print on every chip.`,history:`In 1997 Intel and other chipmakers formed the EUV LLC consortium with three US national laboratories to develop the technology. In 2010 ASML shipped its first pre-production EUV scanner. In 2012 Intel, TSMC and Samsung invested billions of euros in ASML to speed up EUV, and ASML agreed to buy Cymer, the San Diego maker of its light sources. In 2017 ASML's NXE:3400B became the first EUV scanner ready for mass production. In 2019 TSMC's N7+ process became the first to use EUV in volume, for some layers of Huawei's Kirin 990 5G. In 2019 the Dutch government, under US pressure, withheld a licence to ship an EUV machine to China.`,facts:[`EUV scanners fire at about 50,000 tin droplets a second (2019).`,`An NXE:3400 scanner cost around 120 million euros (2019).`]},"tech:highnause":{intro:`High-NA EUV is the next generation of extreme-ultraviolet lithography. By raising the numerical aperture of its optics, a measure of how wide a cone of light they gather, from 0.33 to 0.55, it can print features about 1.7 times smaller in one exposure, avoiding the costly multiple exposures that standard EUV needs for the finest layers.`,how:`Bigger mirrors catch light at steeper angles, which sharpens the image, but they would not fit the standard mask design, so the optics shrink the pattern by different amounts in the two directions: four times one way, eight the other. Each exposure therefore covers only half the usual field, and large chips must be stitched from two halves. The machines are bigger and slower to install, each a project of many months.`,history:`In November 2016 ASML paid one billion euros for a 24.9% stake in Carl Zeiss SMT, its lens maker, partly to fund high-NA optics. In December 2023 ASML shipped the first high-NA EUV scanner, a TWINSCAN EXE:5000, to Intel in Oregon. In April 2024 Intel said it had finished assembling the machine. In February 2025 Intel said it had processed about 30,000 wafers on two high-NA machines. In April 2025 TSMC said it would not need high-NA for its A14 node. Forecasters expected high-NA EUV in volume production around 2027.`,facts:[`A high-NA EUV machine weighs about 150 tonnes (2024).`,`High-NA machines cost about 350 million euros each (2024).`]},"tech:harvest":{intro:`Die harvesting builds chips with more processing units than the product needs, so a chip with a defect in one unit can still be sold with that unit switched off. Big chips almost always contain at least one flaw, and throwing them away would make the largest designs impossibly expensive. Harvesting turns a defect from a dead chip into a slightly smaller product.`,how:`Defects land at random on a wafer, so the chance that a die is perfect falls steeply as its area grows. Designers build in spare cores, cache blocks or memory controllers; after testing, faulty units are permanently disabled by blowing tiny on-chip fuses. Chips are sorted, or “binned”, by how many units work and how fast they run, and sold as different models at different prices from the same silicon.`,history:`In November 2006 Sony's PlayStation 3 launched with IBM's Cell processor, which had eight compute units but used only seven, to improve yields. In 2008 AMD sold three-core Phenom X3 processors made from four-core dies with one core disabled. In 2010 NVIDIA's GTX 480 shipped with 15 of its 16 shader clusters enabled because the full chip yielded so poorly. In April 2016 NVIDIA's Tesla P100 enabled 56 of its 60 multiprocessors. In 2022 the H100 SXM enabled 132 of the full chip's 144.`,facts:[`The A100 enabled 108 of its 128 multiprocessors (2020).`,`The H100 SXM shipped with 132 of 144 multiprocessors working (2022).`]},"tech:yieldai":{intro:`AI yield learning uses machine learning to find and fix the causes of defects in chip factories faster. A new manufacturing process starts with poor yields, perhaps only a fraction of chips working, and engineers spend months tracing each kind of flaw to a faulty step or machine. Deep learning sifts the flood of inspection images and sensor data to point at the culprit in hours.`,how:`Inspection tools photograph wafers after key steps, producing millions of images a day. Neural networks classify each defect, a particle, a scratch, a bridge between wires, far faster and more consistently than human reviewers, while other models match defect maps and electrical test results against readings from the hundreds of sensors on every machine a wafer passed through. Patterns that point to one chamber or one recipe are flagged for engineers to fix.`,history:`In the 1990s chip factories began using automatic defect classification software to sort inspection images by hand-written rules. In 2015 Ming-Ju Wu, Jyh-Shing Roger Jang and Jui-Long Chen published WM-811K, a public set of real wafer maps from a Taiwanese fab, which became a standard test for defect-pattern classifiers. In 2020 TSMC told customers that defects on its 5 nm process were falling faster than they had on 7 nm. In 2021 Applied Materials launched its Enlight inspection system with ExtractAI, which used machine learning to separate real defects from noise.`,facts:[`WM-811K held 811,457 wafer maps (2015).`,`Applied Materials said Enlight inspected wafers about three times faster than before (2021).`]},"tech:cowos":{intro:`CoWoS, short for Chip-on-Wafer-on-Substrate, is TSMC's method of packaging a processor and its memory side by side on a slab of silicon wiring called an interposer, which then sits on an ordinary package substrate. It lets several chips act as one, joined by tens of thousands of fine connections, and it is how the most powerful processors are joined to stacks of HBM memory.`,how:`Finished chips are flipped and attached with tiny solder bumps onto the interposer while it is still part of a whole silicon wafer: the “chip on wafer” step. The interposer carries fine wiring layers and through-silicon vias that pass signals and power down to the package substrate below. The wafer is then cut up and each assembly mounted on its substrate. Capacity is limited and expensive to add, so every chip built this way competes for a share of it.`,history:`In 2011 Xilinx's Virtex-7 2000T, made by TSMC, joined four FPGA slices on one silicon interposer, the technique TSMC named CoWoS. In 2016 NVIDIA's Tesla P100 used CoWoS to join its GPU to four HBM2 stacks. In 2023 demand for NVIDIA's H100 outstripped CoWoS capacity, which became the main limit on how many AI chips could be made. In 2024 TSMC roughly doubled CoWoS capacity and planned to double it again in 2025. In 2024 NVIDIA's Blackwell moved to CoWoS-L, which uses small silicon bridges in an organic interposer to make packages larger than any single slab of silicon.`,facts:[`In 2024 TSMC planned CoWoS packages of 5.5 times the reticle limit within two years.`,`The P100's interposer joined one GPU and 16 GB of HBM2 (2016).`]},"tech:hybridbond":{intro:`Hybrid bonding joins two chips face to face by fusing their copper contacts, and the glassy insulator around them, directly, with no solder bumps in between. The connections can be a few micrometres apart instead of tens, so thousands more fit in each square millimetre. Stacked this way, two dies behave almost like one, letting cache be piled on top of processor cores.`,how:`Both surfaces are polished flat to within a nanometre, with the copper pads recessed very slightly. They are pressed together at room temperature, and the oxide layers stick by chemical bonds; heating then makes the copper expand and fuse into solid metal joints. Any particle of dust leaves a void that ruins the bond, so the process needs cleanroom conditions like those of a chip fab, not a packaging plant.`,history:`In 2016 Sony's camera sensor for Samsung's Galaxy S7 used direct copper bonding to join the layer of pixels to its processing layer. In June 2021 AMD announced 3D V-Cache, made with TSMC's SoIC hybrid bonding. In April 2022 AMD's Ryzen 7 5800X3D went on sale with 64 MB of extra cache stacked on its processor die. In December 2023 AMD's MI300 stacked compute dies on input-output dies by hybrid bonding. In 2024 memory makers tested hybrid bonding for future HBM stacks of 16 or more dies.`,facts:[`The 5800X3D's bonds were 9 micrometres apart, about 15 times denser than micro-bumps (2022).`,`The 5800X3D carried 96 MB of L3 cache in all (2022).`]},"tech:euvsource":{intro:`Building your own EUV lithography machine means mastering one of the most complex machines ever sold: a light source that vaporises molten tin droplets with a laser, mirrors polished smoother than almost anything else humans make, and stages that move wafers with nanometre precision at high speed. Only one company, ASML, has ever brought one to market, relying on hundreds of specialist suppliers.`,how:`A carbon dioxide laser fires two pulses at each tin droplet, about 50,000 times a second: the first flattens it into a pancake, the second turns it into a plasma that glows at 13.5 nanometres. A collector mirror gathers the light, and a train of molybdenum and silicon multilayer mirrors, made by Zeiss, steers it via a reflective mask onto the wafer, all in vacuum. Keeping tin debris off the optics and producing enough power are the hardest parts.`,history:`In 1986 Hiroo Kinoshita at NTT in Japan demonstrated projection lithography with soft X-rays, the light later called extreme ultraviolet. In 2006 ASML shipped two EUV demonstration tools to research centres in Albany, New York, and Leuven, Belgium. In 2019 TSMC and Samsung began using EUV in volume production, while US pressure stopped ASML shipping EUV machines to China. In December 2025 Reuters reported that a team in Shenzhen, including former ASML engineers, had built a prototype EUV machine that generated EUV light but had not yet made working chips. Forecasters expected a second maker of EUV tools to emerge around 2030.`,facts:[`An ASML EUV scanner contains about 100,000 parts (2019).`,`Each EUV machine ships in about 40 freight containers (2019).`]},"tech:tsv":{intro:`A through-silicon via, or TSV, is a vertical wire that runs straight through a silicon chip, from its top surface to its bottom. Thousands of them in a thinned chip let several chips be stacked like the floors of a building, connected directly through their bodies instead of by wires looping round the edges. It makes stacked memory possible.`,how:`Deep, narrow holes, a few micrometres wide and tens deep, are etched into the wafer, lined with insulator and filled with copper. The wafer is then ground down from the back until the copper ends poke through, leaving it only about 50 micrometres thick. Tiny solder micro-bumps connect each via to the chip above. The vias take up space and strain the silicon around them, so their number and placement must be planned with care.`,history:`In 1958 the transistor pioneer William Shockley filed a patent describing holes etched through a semiconductor wafer, an early form of the idea. In October 2011 Micron and Samsung founded the Hybrid Memory Cube Consortium to promote stacks of DRAM joined by TSVs. In October 2013 the standards body JEDEC published HBM, a stacked memory standard built on TSVs. In August 2014 Samsung began mass-producing 64 GB server memory modules made of DDR4 chips stacked with TSVs.`,facts:[`JEDEC's HBM standard was numbered JESD235 (2013).`,`A wafer thinned for stacking is about 50 micrometres thick, half a human hair (2013).`]},"tech:m_hbm1":{intro:`High Bandwidth Memory stacks several DRAM chips on top of a base logic chip and joins them with through-silicon vias, making a compact tower of memory with an extremely wide interface. Instead of the 32 data wires of a graphics memory chip, an HBM stack has 1,024. Placed right beside the processor, it delivers far more data per second for less power than conventional graphics memory.`,how:`The first-generation stack is four DRAM dies of 2 gigabits each, giving 1 GB, on a base die that handles the interface. Its 1,024 wires, split into eight independent channels, each run at only about 1 gigabit a second, so the signals are easy and cool, yet together they move 128 GB a second. The stack sits on a silicon interposer beside the processor, because an ordinary circuit board could not route so many connections.`,history:`In 2013 SK hynix developed the first HBM chips with AMD, and in October 2013 JEDEC adopted HBM as a standard. In June 2015 AMD's Radeon R9 Fury X became the first product with HBM, using four SK hynix stacks. In 2015 AMD said HBM gave over three times the bandwidth per watt of GDDR5. In 2015 the Fury X was limited to 4 GB of memory, because each first-generation stack held only 1 GB, while rival cards offered 6 GB. In January 2016 JEDEC defined HBM2, which soon replaced it.`,facts:[`A first-generation HBM stack held 1 GB and moved 128 GB a second (2015).`,`AMD measured over 35 GB a second per watt for HBM, against about 10.7 for GDDR5 (2015).`]},"tech:m_hbm2":{intro:`HBM2 is the second generation of High Bandwidth Memory. It keeps the 1,024-wire interface but doubles the speed of each wire and allows taller stacks of bigger chips, so one stack holds up to 8 GB and delivers 256 GB a second. That made the memory practical for large data-centre processors, where capacity matters as much as speed.`,how:`Each data pin runs at up to 2 gigabits a second, twice the first generation's rate. Dies of 8 gigabits, stacked two, four or eight high, give 2, 4 or 8 GB per stack. A “pseudo-channel” mode splits each channel in two so the wide bus stays busy with smaller requests. Support for error-correcting codes made it fit for servers and supercomputers, where a flipped bit can spoil a long calculation.`,history:`In January 2016 JEDEC updated its HBM standard to define HBM2, and Samsung began mass-producing 4 GB stacks, the first in the industry. In April 2016 NVIDIA's Tesla P100 used four Samsung HBM2 stacks. In 2017 SK hynix began volume production of HBM2, and AMD's Radeon RX Vega and NVIDIA's V100 used it. In January 2018 Samsung's Aquabolt HBM2 raised speeds to 2.4 gigabits per pin.`,facts:[`The P100's four HBM2 stacks gave 16 GB and 720 GB a second (2016).`,`Samsung's Aquabolt moved 307 GB a second per stack (2018).`]},"tech:m_hbm2e":{intro:`HBM2e is an extended version of HBM2 that stacks eight memory dies and runs them faster, giving 16 GB and roughly 400 GB a second per stack. It arrived as AI models were outgrowing the memory of any single processor, and the extra capacity and speed let accelerators keep bigger models and batches close at hand.`,how:`The dies grew to 16 gigabits each, stacked eight high on the base die for 16 GB. Data pins run at 3.2 to 3.6 gigabits a second, so the stack's 1,024 wires move about 410 to 460 GB a second. Thinner dies and finer through-silicon vias keep an eight-high stack within the same height limit as earlier ones, so it fits the same package designs.`,history:`In March 2019 Samsung announced Flashbolt, its HBM2e, at 3.2 gigabits a second per pin. In August 2019 SK hynix announced its own HBM2e at 3.6 gigabits per pin. In July 2020 SK hynix began mass production of HBM2e. In November 2020 NVIDIA released an 80 GB version of its A100 using HBM2e. In November 2021 AMD's MI250X carried 128 GB of HBM2e across eight stacks. In January 2023 Intel's Xeon Max became the first x86 server processor with HBM2e built into its package.`,facts:[`SK hynix's HBM2e moved 460 GB a second per stack (2019).`,`The A100 80 GB used five active HBM2e stacks for about 2 TB a second (2020).`]},"tech:m_hbm3":{intro:`HBM3 is the third generation of High Bandwidth Memory, built for the largest AI accelerators. It nearly doubles the speed of each wire over HBM2e and doubles the number of independent channels, giving about 819 GB a second per stack. Stacks of up to twelve dies hold up to 24 GB, so a processor ringed with several stacks has around 100 GB within millimetres.`,how:`Each pin runs at 6.4 gigabits a second, and the 1,024-bit interface is split into 16 channels, each divided into two pseudo-channels, so more requests can be served at once. Stacks of 8 or 12 dies of 16 gigabits give 16 or 24 GB. Error correction on each die and a lower signalling voltage keep reliability and power in check at the higher speed. Building tall stacks with good yield is hard, so supply is tight.`,history:`In October 2021 SK hynix announced it had developed the first HBM3. In January 2022 JEDEC published the HBM3 standard. In June 2022 SK hynix began mass production of HBM3, with NVIDIA's H100 as its first customer. In 2023 demand for H100s made HBM a bottleneck, and SK hynix, with its early lead, became NVIDIA's main supplier. In December 2023 AMD's MI300X carried 192 GB of HBM3 in eight stacks.`,facts:[`An HBM3 stack moved about 819 GB a second (2022).`,`The H100 SXM used five HBM3 stacks for 80 GB and 3.35 TB a second (2022).`,`AMD's MI300X moved 5.3 TB a second from its HBM3 (2023).`]},"tech:m_hbm3e":{intro:`HBM3e is an extended HBM3 that pushes each wire to around 9 gigabits a second and stacks up to twelve dies, giving 36 GB and over a terabyte a second in a single stack. It is made by just three companies, and each new AI accelerator is judged as much by how many stacks it carries as by its own speed.`,how:`Twelve DRAM dies, each thinner than a sheet of paper, are stacked within the same height limit as earlier eight-high stacks. SK hynix fills the gaps between dies with a moulded underfill, MR-MUF, that carries heat away well, while Samsung and Micron use a thin non-conductive film. Heat, warping and yield losses grow with every added layer, so twelve-high stacks are the hardest to make.`,history:`In 2023 SK hynix, Micron and Samsung each announced HBM3e. In February 2024 Micron began mass production of eight-high HBM3e for NVIDIA's H200, and in March 2024 SK hynix followed. In September 2024 SK hynix began mass-producing twelve-high, 36 GB stacks. In 2024 Samsung struggled to pass NVIDIA's qualification tests for its HBM3e. In early 2025 SK hynix overtook Samsung in DRAM revenue for the first time, thanks largely to HBM.`,facts:[`An H200 held 141 GB of HBM3e moving 4.8 TB a second (2024).`,`A B200 carried 192 GB of HBM3e at 8 TB a second (2024).`,`SK hynix's twelve-high stacks held 36 GB each (2024).`]},"tech:m_hbm4":{intro:`HBM4 is the fourth generation of High Bandwidth Memory. It doubles the interface to 2,048 wires per stack, so even at similar wire speeds a stack moves about twice as much data, over 2 TB a second. For the first time the base die at the bottom can be made by a logic chipmaker such as TSMC, rather than in the memory maker's own fab.`,how:`Doubling the wires needs twice as many connections through the interposer and 32 independent channels per stack instead of 16. Stacks of 12 or 16 dies hold 36 to 64 GB. Moving the base die to a logic process gives room for faster input-output circuits and, for some customers, their own custom logic, blurring the line between memory chip and processor and tying memory makers more closely to foundries.`,history:`In April 2024 SK hynix and TSMC agreed to work together on HBM4 base dies. In March 2025 SK hynix shipped the first HBM4 samples to customers. In April 2025 JEDEC published the HBM4 standard, JESD270-4, with speeds of up to 8 gigabits per pin. In September 2025 SK hynix said it had finished developing HBM4 and was ready for mass production. In 2026 NVIDIA's Vera Rubin GPUs carried HBM4, giving each GPU roughly 20 TB a second of memory bandwidth.`,facts:[`JEDEC's HBM4 standard allowed up to 64 GB and 2 TB a second per stack (2025).`,`SK hynix's HBM4 ran at over 10 gigabits a second per pin (2025).`]},"tech:m_hbm4e":{intro:`HBM4e is the planned extended version of HBM4, with faster wires and stacks of up to sixteen dies holding 64 GB or more. Its defining feature is the custom base die: chip customers such as NVIDIA, Google or Amazon can have their own logic built into the bottom layer of the memory stack, made at a foundry, turning memory into a semi-custom part of each accelerator.`,how:`A customer designs, or co-designs with the memory maker, a base die that might hold memory controllers, data compression or other functions normally on the processor, freeing room on the main chip. Sixteen dies must fit in a stack whose height the standard limits, so dies are thinned further. Hybrid bonding, fusing copper pads directly with no bumps, may replace micro-bumps between the dies to save height and carry heat away better.`,history:`In 2024 SK hynix and Samsung said they would offer customised base dies from HBM4 onwards. In 2024 JEDEC relaxed the maximum stack height to 775 micrometres, so sixteen-high stacks could be built with existing bonding methods. In March 2025 NVIDIA said its Rubin Ultra GPUs, two years away, would carry 1 TB of HBM4e. Forecasters expected HBM4e in products around 2027.`,facts:[`NVIDIA planned 1 TB of HBM4e for each Rubin Ultra package (2025).`,`JEDEC set the maximum HBM4 stack height at 775 micrometres (2024).`]},"tech:m_hbm5":{intro:`HBM5 is a projected future generation of High Bandwidth Memory, expected to stack around twenty dies joined by hybrid bonding and to deliver several terabytes a second per stack. Forecasts point to wider interfaces, more of the processor's work moved into the base die, and cooling built into the stack itself, as memory becomes the main limit on AI performance.`,how:`Hybrid bonding, fusing copper to copper without solder bumps, would let twenty or more dies stack within the height limit and carry heat away better. An interface perhaps 4,096 bits wide would double bandwidth again. With so much heat trapped in a tall stack, designers expect to need cooling channels or liquid running close to the memory, and to design memory, base die and processor together as one system.`,history:`In 2023 SK hynix said it was developing hybrid bonding for future HBM generations. In 2024 Samsung said it had built test HBM stacks of sixteen dies with hybrid bonding. In June 2025 researchers at KAIST in South Korea published a roadmap projecting HBM5 with twice HBM4's interface width, about 4 TB a second per stack and cooling built into the stack. Forecasters expected HBM5 around 2030.`,facts:[`HBM's interface had doubled once, from 1,024 to 2,048 bits, by 2025.`,`A single HBM4 stack moved about 2 TB a second (2025).`]},"tech:m_3d":{intro:`3D DRAM would build memory cells in hundreds of layers on a single chip, as flash memory already does. Each cell is laid on its side and the cells are stacked vertically, so one die could hold many times more than a conventional DRAM chip, whose cells sit in a single flat layer across the silicon.`,how:`A DRAM cell is a transistor and a tiny capacitor that holds a charge for each bit. Flat cells are near the limit of how small they can be made. In 3D designs the cells are turned horizontal and stacked in layers, with vertical wires running through them, much as 3D flash stacks its cells. Some proposals drop the capacitor altogether and store charge in the transistor's body. Etching and filling such deep, narrow structures evenly is the challenge.`,history:`In 2013 Samsung began mass-producing V-NAND, 3D flash memory with 24 layers of cells. In November 2024 SK hynix began mass production of 321-layer flash memory, while DRAM cells were still built in a single layer. In May 2023 the start-up NEO Semiconductor proposed 3D X-DRAM, a design adapted from 3D flash. In 2024 Samsung said at its Memcon conference that it was developing vertically stacked DRAM. Forecasters expected 3D DRAM in production around 2033.`,facts:[`Samsung's first 3D flash had 24 layers (2013).`,`SK hynix's flash reached 321 layers (2024).`]},"tech:cxl":{intro:`CXL, Compute Express Link, is an open standard for connecting processors, accelerators and memory over the same physical slots as PCI Express, but able to share memory coherently. With CXL memory pooling, servers in a rack can draw on a shared bank of memory as they need it, instead of each being stuck with whatever is plugged into its own sockets.`,how:`CXL runs over PCI Express wiring but adds protocols that let a processor read and write memory on a CXL device with ordinary instructions, keeping caches consistent. A CXL memory expander adds DRAM to a server beyond its built-in channels. Version 2.0 added switches so several hosts can share a pool, and version 3.0 added larger fabrics and true sharing between hosts. Access is slower than local memory, roughly like reaching the memory of another processor socket.`,history:`In March 2019 Intel and partners including Alibaba, Cisco, Dell, Facebook, Google, HPE, Huawei and Microsoft announced CXL 1.0. In November 2020 CXL 2.0 added switching and memory pooling. In August 2022 CXL 3.0 doubled the speed and allowed multi-level fabrics, and in 2022 the rival Gen-Z and OpenCAPI groups folded their work into CXL. In November 2022 AMD's Genoa and in January 2023 Intel's Sapphire Rapids server processors became the first to support CXL memory expansion. In 2023 Microsoft researchers estimated that pooling could cut a cloud provider's memory needs by about 7%.`,facts:[`CXL 3.0 ran at 64 gigatransfers a second per lane (2022).`,`Memory could be half the cost of a cloud server, Microsoft researchers said (2023).`]},"tech:pim":{intro:`Processing in memory puts small computing units inside the memory chips themselves, so simple calculations happen where the data lives instead of after a long trip to the processor. For AI inference, which spends most of its time and energy fetching weights from memory, doing part of the arithmetic inside the DRAM could save power and multiply the effective bandwidth.`,how:`Beside the arrays of storage cells in each memory bank, a few simple arithmetic units multiply and add data as it is read, using the bank's full internal bandwidth, many times what reaches the chip's pins. The processor sends commands and the memory returns results rather than raw data. The units must be built on a DRAM process, which makes slow, leaky logic, and software has to split the work between processor and memory.`,history:`In 1997 David Patterson's group at Berkeley proposed IRAM, putting a processor and DRAM on the same chip. In 2019 the French start-up UPMEM launched DRAM modules with simple processors inside each memory chip. In February 2021 Samsung announced HBM-PIM, with AI engines inside each memory bank. In February 2022 SK hynix showed GDDR6-AiM, short for accelerator in memory. Forecasters expected processing in memory in mainstream AI chips around 2028.`,facts:[`Samsung claimed HBM-PIM doubled performance while cutting energy by over 70% (2021).`,`SK hynix said GDDR6-AiM ran some calculations up to 16 times faster (2022).`]},"tech:liquid":{intro:`Liquid cooling carries heat away from chips with water or another coolant instead of air. Water holds about 3,500 times as much heat per litre as air, so a slim pipe can do the work of a gale of fans. It lets far more powerful chips be packed tightly together, beyond the point where air cooling becomes deafening and useless.`,how:`In direct-to-chip cooling, a copper cold plate with fine internal channels sits on each processor and coolant flows through it, then through pipes to a coolant distribution unit, which passes the heat to the building's water loop and on to cooling towers or dry coolers. Warm-water systems run at 30 to 45 degrees and need no chillers. An alternative submerges whole servers in tanks of non-conducting fluid. Leaks and corrosion in a room full of electronics are the risks.`,history:`In 1985 the Cray-2 supercomputer was cooled by immersing its circuit boards in Fluorinert, an inert liquid that bubbled as it carried heat away. In 2012 the SuperMUC supercomputer near Munich was cooled with warm water at about 40 degrees, saving the energy of chillers. In May 2018 Google announced that its TPU v3 pods were liquid-cooled, a first for its data centres. In 2024 NVIDIA's GB200 NVL72 racks required direct liquid cooling. In December 2024 Microsoft announced data centre designs that recycle their cooling water in a closed loop instead of evaporating it.`,facts:[`The Cray-2 was nicknamed “Bubbles” for its bubbling coolant (1985).`,`A GB200 NVL72 rack needed liquid to remove about 120 kilowatts of heat (2024).`]},"tech:rapidbuild":{intro:`Rapid construction is the art of building a data centre in months instead of years. Prefabricated power and cooling modules arrive on lorries ready to plug in, temporary generators and turbines supply electricity before the grid connection is ready, and crews work in shifts around the clock. In an AI race where a few months' head start matters, speed is worth paying for.`,how:`Instead of designing each building from scratch, builders repeat standard designs and make electrical rooms, cooling units and even whole server halls in factories, then assemble them on site. Existing industrial buildings can be converted rather than built new. When the grid cannot supply power soon enough, mobile gas turbines and banks of batteries fill the gap. Permits, power equipment such as large transformers, and skilled electricians are the bottlenecks.`,history:`In 2023 the rush for AI computing left buyers waiting years for large transformers and grid connections in many regions. In 2024 Elon Musk's xAI converted a former Electrolux factory in Memphis, Tennessee, into the Colossus supercomputer with 100,000 NVIDIA H100s. In 2024 xAI powered Colossus partly with dozens of mobile gas turbines, drawing complaints from local residents about air pollution. In 2025 xAI said it had doubled Colossus to 200,000 GPUs. In 2025 Meta housed AI servers in large temporary tents while permanent buildings were finished.`,facts:[`xAI built Colossus with 100,000 GPUs in 122 days (2024).`,`xAI said it doubled Colossus in a further 92 days (2025).`]},"tech:medvision":{intro:`Medical image AI uses deep learning to read medical scans: photographs of the back of the eye, chest X-rays, mammograms, skin lesions and pathology slides. Trained on many thousands of images labelled by specialists, a network can spot signs of disease as accurately as experienced doctors at some tasks, screening more patients faster where specialists are scarce.`,how:`A convolutional network, often first trained on everyday photos, is fine-tuned on scans each graded by several specialists, whose agreed grade becomes the right answer. It outputs a probability of disease, and a threshold is chosen to favour either catching cases or avoiding false alarms. Systems must be tested on patients from other hospitals and machines, where accuracy often drops, and approved by regulators before use.`,history:`In 1998 the US Food and Drug Administration approved R2 Technology's ImageChecker, a computer-aided detection system for mammograms using older hand-designed methods. In December 2016 Varun Gulshan and colleagues at Google reported in the journal JAMA a network that detected diabetic eye disease as accurately as eye specialists. In January 2017 Stanford researchers showed in Nature a network that classified skin cancers as well as 21 dermatologists. In April 2018 the FDA authorised IDx-DR, the first autonomous AI diagnostic system, which screens for diabetic eye disease with no doctor reading the image. In January 2020 a Google Health system for breast cancer screening, published in Nature, missed fewer cancers than radiologists in tests.`,facts:[`Google's eye model trained on about 128,000 retinal images (2016).`,`Google's mammogram system cut missed cancers by 9.4% on US data (2020).`,`The FDA had authorised over 1,000 AI-enabled medical devices by 2025, most in radiology.`]},"tech:fastmri":{intro:`AI MRI reconstruction uses neural networks to make clear MRI images from far less data, so scans take a fraction of the time. An MRI scan is slow because the machine gathers its measurements one line at a time while the patient lies still; skip most of the lines and the image fills with artefacts. A trained network fills in what is missing.`,how:`An MRI scanner measures radio signals from hydrogen nuclei in a strong magnetic field, sampling the image's frequency content, called k-space, line by line. Collecting only, say, every fourth line speeds up the scan but leaves gaps. A network trained on thousands of fully sampled scans learns to rebuild a clean image from partial data while staying consistent with the measurements actually taken. Radiologists must check that it neither invents nor erases details.`,history:`In December 2007 Michael Lustig, David Donoho and John Pauly at Stanford applied compressed sensing to MRI, rebuilding images from fewer measurements by mathematical optimisation. In August 2018 Facebook AI Research and NYU Langone Health launched fastMRI, releasing the largest public collection of raw MRI data. In 2020 an NYU study in the American Journal of Roentgenology found radiologists judged knee images from four-times-faster AI scans interchangeable with standard ones. Between 2020 and 2022 GE Healthcare, Siemens Healthineers and Philips each won FDA clearance for deep learning MRI reconstruction.`,facts:[`fastMRI's first release held raw data from about 1,500 knee scans (2018).`,`The AI-accelerated knee scans ran four times faster than standard ones (2020).`]},"tech:generchem":{intro:`Generative chemistry uses AI models to invent new drug molecules rather than search through existing ones. A chemist describes the properties wanted, such as binding a particular protein, dissolving in blood and not being toxic, and the model proposes molecules that have never been made, which chemists then synthesise and test. It aims to shorten the years spent finding a promising compound.`,how:`Molecules are written as strings of text, such as SMILES notation, or as graphs of atoms and bonds. A generative network, a variational autoencoder, a GAN or a language model, learns from millions of known molecules what plausible chemistry looks like. Reinforcement learning or optimisation in the model's internal space then steers its output toward molecules predicted to have the target properties. Many suggestions are impractical to make, so filters and chemists' judgement prune them.`,history:`In 2016 Rafael Gómez-Bombarelli, Alán Aspuru-Guzik and colleagues at Harvard showed that a variational autoencoder could map molecules into a continuous space and optimise them there. In September 2019 Insilico Medicine, led by Alex Zhavoronkov, reported in Nature Biotechnology that its GENTRL system had designed new inhibitors of the DDR1 enzyme, one of which worked in mice. In January 2020 Exscientia and Sumitomo Dainippon Pharma put DSP-1181 into human trials, billed as the first AI-designed drug tested in people. In June 2025 Insilico published in Nature Medicine phase 2a results for rentosertib, its AI-discovered drug for lung fibrosis.`,facts:[`GENTRL designed its molecules in 21 days, and the whole project took 46 (2019).`,`DSP-1181's discovery took under 12 months, against a typical four to five years (2020).`]},"tech:antibioticml":{intro:`Deep molecule screening uses a neural network to predict which molecules will have a desired effect, such as killing bacteria, and then runs that prediction over enormous virtual libraries far beyond what any laboratory could physically test. The few most promising candidates go to the lab. It turns a needle-in-a-haystack search into a fast sweep by computer.`,how:`Scientists first test a few thousand molecules in the lab, measuring which ones stop bacteria growing. A graph neural network, which reads a molecule as atoms joined by bonds, learns from these results to predict antibacterial activity from structure alone. It then scores hundreds of millions of virtual molecules. The top hits are filtered for novelty, so they are unlike existing antibiotics, and for predicted toxicity, then tested in dishes and in mice.`,history:`In February 2017 the World Health Organization published a list of bacteria resistant to existing antibiotics for which new drugs were urgently needed. In February 2020 Jonathan Stokes, Regina Barzilay and James Collins at MIT reported in the journal Cell that a network had found halicin, a molecule that killed many drug-resistant bacteria. In 2020 halicin, once studied as a possible diabetes drug, was named after HAL, the computer in the film 2001: A Space Odyssey. In May 2023 the same group found abaucin, which targets Acinetobacter baumannii, a hospital superbug. In December 2023 they reported in Nature a new class of antibiotics against MRSA, found with explainable deep learning. In August 2025 Collins's lab used generative AI to design new antibiotics against gonorrhoea and MRSA.`,facts:[`The halicin network learned from about 2,300 molecules tested against E coli (2020).`,`It then scored more than 107 million molecules (2020).`]},"tech:foldpredict":{intro:`Protein structure prediction works out the three-dimensional shape a protein folds into from its sequence of amino acids alone. Shape decides what a protein does, how it binds partners, speeds up reactions or can be blocked by a drug, but measuring it by X-ray crystallography can take a year or more for each protein. Predicting it accurately by computer had been an open problem for half a century.`,how:`AlphaFold 2 starts from the protein's sequence and an alignment of related sequences from other species, since amino acids that mutate together tend to touch in the folded shape. A Transformer-style network, the Evoformer, repeatedly refines its picture of which residues lie near each other, and a structure module turns that into 3D coordinates for every atom. It also reports its own confidence for each part of the prediction.`,history:`In 1972 Christian Anfinsen won a Nobel prize for showing that a protein's sequence determines its shape. In 1994 John Moult and Krzysztof Fidelis started CASP, a contest held every two years in which teams predict structures that have been measured but not yet published. In December 2018 DeepMind's first AlphaFold won CASP13. In November 2020 AlphaFold 2 predicted structures at CASP14 close to experimental accuracy. In July 2021 DeepMind and the European Bioinformatics Institute opened a free database of predicted structures. In October 2024 Demis Hassabis and John Jumper shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for AlphaFold.`,facts:[`AlphaFold 2's median accuracy score at CASP14 was 92.4 out of 100 (2020).`,`The AlphaFold database held over 200 million predicted structures (2022).`,`Experiments had solved about 170,000 protein structures by 2020.`]},"tech:protlm":{intro:`A protein language model is trained on millions of protein sequences the way a chatbot is trained on text: by hiding some amino acids and learning to guess them. Without being told anything about structure or function, it picks up the “grammar” of proteins, which positions matter and which pairs touch, and that knowledge can predict shapes and the effects of mutations, and even propose new proteins.`,how:`Each of the 20 amino acids is a token, and a protein is a sentence hundreds of tokens long. A Transformer reads millions of natural sequences, filling in masked positions. Its internal attention patterns come to reflect which residues touch in the folded protein. A small structure module on top, as in ESMFold, predicts 3D shapes from a single sequence in seconds, skipping the slow search for related sequences that AlphaFold relies on.`,history:`In April 2019 Alexander Rives and colleagues at Facebook AI Research showed that a Transformer trained on 250 million protein sequences learned biological properties by itself. In 2020 Ali Madani and colleagues at Salesforce introduced ProGen, which generated new protein sequences, and in January 2023 they reported ProGen-designed enzymes that worked in the lab. In 2022 Meta released ESM-2 and ESMFold, which predicted structures up to 60 times faster than AlphaFold 2. In November 2022 Meta published the ESM Metagenomic Atlas of proteins from microbes in soil, sea water and the human gut. In June 2024 former Meta researchers at the start-up EvolutionaryScale released ESM3 and used it to design a new glowing protein.`,facts:[`ESM-2's largest version had 15 billion parameters (2022).`,`The ESM Metagenomic Atlas held 617 million predicted structures (2022).`,`ESM3's new glowing protein was as different as 500 million years of evolution, its makers estimated (2024).`]},"tech:protdiffusion":{intro:`Protein design by diffusion generates brand-new protein shapes, ones not found in nature, with the same technique image generators use to make pictures from noise. A designer can ask for a protein that grips a particular virus protein or hormone, and the model produces backbones shaped to fit; other tools then choose sequences that will fold that way. Many designs work in the lab on the first try.`,how:`RFdiffusion starts from a cloud of randomly placed amino acid positions and, step by step, denoises them into a plausible protein backbone, guided by conditions such as “bind this site” or “form a ring of three copies”. It was built by fine-tuning the RoseTTAFold structure predictor as the denoiser. ProteinMPNN then designs an amino acid sequence for the backbone, and AlphaFold checks that the sequence really folds into the intended shape before anything is made.`,history:`In 2003 David Baker's lab at the University of Washington designed Top7, a protein with a fold never seen in nature, using its Rosetta software. In July 2021 Baker's group released RoseTTAFold, an open structure predictor rivalling AlphaFold 2. In September 2022 Baker's lab published ProteinMPNN for designing sequences. In December 2022 Joseph Watson, David Juergens and colleagues in Baker's lab posted RFdiffusion, and in July 2023 Nature published it. In October 2024 Baker shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for computational protein design. In January 2025 Baker's group reported designed proteins that protected mice from deadly snake venom toxins.`,facts:[`Top7, the first protein with an invented fold, had 93 amino acids (2003).`,`Baker won half the 2024 chemistry Nobel; Hassabis and Jumper shared the other half.`]},"tech:neoantigen":{intro:`A personalised cancer vaccine is made for one patient from the mutations in their own tumour. Cancer cells carry altered proteins, called neoantigens, that the immune system could recognise as foreign if shown them clearly. Sequencing the tumour, choosing the most visible mutations by computer and encoding them in an mRNA vaccine trains the patient's immune cells to hunt down any cancer left after surgery.`,how:`The tumour and a blood sample are both DNA-sequenced to find mutations present only in the cancer. Algorithms predict which mutated protein fragments the patient's own immune molecules will display on the cell surface, and which will provoke a response; the best few dozen are chosen. A single mRNA strand encoding them all is made within weeks and injected, usually alongside a drug that releases the brakes on the immune system.`,history:`In 2016 Moderna and Merck agreed to develop personalised cancer vaccines together. In July 2017 Ugur Sahin's team at BioNTech and Catherine Wu's group at Dana-Farber separately reported in Nature that personalised neoantigen vaccines triggered immune responses in melanoma patients. In December 2022 Moderna and Merck reported that their vaccine, mRNA-4157, given with Merck's Keytruda, cut the risk of melanoma returning or death compared with Keytruda alone in a phase 2b trial. In May 2023 a study at Memorial Sloan Kettering found a BioNTech personalised vaccine prompted immune responses in half of 16 pancreatic cancer patients. In August 2026 Merck and Moderna said the phase 3 trial of the vaccine, named intismeran autogene, had met its main goal of delaying melanoma's return.`,facts:[`Each vaccine encoded up to 34 of the patient's tumour mutations (2022).`,`The phase 2b trial showed a 44% lower risk of relapse or death (2022).`,`The phase 3 trial enrolled 1,137 melanoma patients (2026).`]},"tech:lineardesign":{intro:`mRNA sequence design chooses the exact genetic spelling of an mRNA vaccine or medicine. Because most amino acids can be encoded by several different three-letter codons, the same protein can be written in an astronomical number of ways, and the choice affects how stable the mRNA is and how much protein it makes. Algorithms search this vast space for spellings that fold tightly and last longer.`,how:`An mRNA strand folds back on itself into hairpins and loops; tighter, more stable structures resist breaking down, so the vaccine survives longer and makes more protein. LinearDesign borrows from the study of language: it treats choosing codons like finding the most likely sentence under a grammar, building a “lattice” of all the codon options and using a fast parsing algorithm to find the most stable folding sequence, while still favouring codons that cells read efficiently.`,history:`In 2005 Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman at the University of Pennsylvania showed that modified building blocks let mRNA avoid triggering inflammation, a key to mRNA medicines. In December 2020 the first mRNA vaccines against COVID-19, from Pfizer and BioNTech and from Moderna, were authorised. In May 2023 Liang Huang and colleagues at Baidu Research published LinearDesign in Nature, showing it found an optimised spike protein mRNA in 11 minutes. In October 2023 Karikó and Weissman won the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.`,facts:[`The COVID spike protein can be spelled in about 10 to the power 632 mRNA sequences (2023).`,`LinearDesign's mRNAs raised antibody levels in mice up to 128 times (2023).`]},"tech:dnalm":{intro:`A genomic language model is trained on DNA itself, reading long stretches of genetic letters the way a chatbot reads text. From the patterns alone it learns which parts of a genome tend to stay the same across species, where genes begin and end, and which changes look unusual, so biologists can ask it which variants in a patient's genome are likely to matter.`,how:`The hard part is length: a gene and its control regions can span hundreds of thousands of letters, far more than an ordinary Transformer can read. Models such as Evo mix attention with long convolution filters so they can take in huge stretches at single-letter resolution. Their main scientific use is scoring: how surprising the model finds a change hints at whether it would disturb how the organism works.`,history:`In 2021 Yanrong Ji and colleagues' DNABERT applied the BERT language model to DNA. In January 2023 InstaDeep and NVIDIA released the Nucleotide Transformer, trained on 3,202 human genomes and the genomes of 850 other species. In February 2024 the Arc Institute and Stanford, led by Brian Hie and Patrick Hsu, released Evo, trained on millions of microbial genomes. In February 2025 Science published Evo 2, with 40 billion parameters, trained on 9.3 trillion DNA letters from across the tree of life, and released free with its training data. In 2025 Evo 2 predicted which mutations in the BRCA1 breast cancer gene are harmful with about 90% accuracy.`,facts:[`Evo 2 read up to a million DNA letters at once (2025).`,`Evo 2 trained on 9.3 trillion DNA letters from over 100,000 species (2025).`]},"tech:allatom":{intro:`All-molecule structure prediction goes beyond proteins alone and predicts how proteins, DNA, RNA, small drug molecules, metal ions and sugar chains sit together as a complex. Most biology happens in such mixtures, and most medicines are small molecules clamped into a pocket of a protein, so predicting the whole assembly is far more useful to drug researchers than predicting one chain at a time.`,how:`AlphaFold 3 replaced the earlier structure module with a diffusion network that works directly on atoms, denoising a cloud of positions into a finished assembly, so it is not limited to the fixed backbone shape of an amino acid chain. A single network handles every kind of molecule rather than a separate tool for each. Its confidence scores tell researchers which parts to trust, and accuracy is lower for flexible molecules and unusual chemistry.`,history:`In May 2024 Google DeepMind and Isomorphic Labs published AlphaFold 3 in Nature, predicting complexes of proteins with DNA, RNA, ions and small molecules. In May 2024 the authors reported it was about 50% more accurate than the best existing methods on protein and drug-molecule complexes. In 2024 the model's code was at first withheld, drawing criticism from scientists, and in November 2024 DeepMind released it for academic use. In March 2024 the start-up Chai Discovery, and in 2024 the Baker lab's RoseTTAFold All-Atom, published comparable all-molecule models. In 2025 a free server let researchers run AlphaFold 3 predictions without their own computers.`,facts:[`AlphaFold 3 was about 50% more accurate on protein and drug-molecule pairs (2024).`,`Isomorphic Labs was founded as a DeepMind drug discovery spin-off in 2021.`]},"tech:virtualcell":{intro:`A virtual cell model tries to predict how a living cell will respond to something done to it, such as a drug or a gene switched off, without doing the experiment. Trained on millions of measurements of single cells, it learns the patterns of which genes are active together, so a biologist can test thousands of ideas by computer and take only the most promising to the laboratory.`,how:`Single-cell sequencing measures the activity of thousands of genes in one cell at a time, giving each cell a long list of numbers. A model learns from many such lists, paired with the treatment each cell received, to predict the list that a given cell type would show after a given change. Judging these models is difficult: simply predicting that nothing much changes scores surprisingly well, so benchmarks must be designed to catch that.`,history:`In 2001 Masaru Tomita's group at Keio University in Japan published E-CELL, an early attempt to simulate a whole cell by equations. In July 2012 Markus Covert's group at Stanford published a simulation of the entire life cycle of the tiny bacterium Mycoplasma genitalium. In December 2023 Theodoris and colleagues' Geneformer and other “foundation models” for single-cell data appeared, trained on tens of millions of cells. In December 2024 a group of scientists led by the Chan Zuckerberg Initiative published a call for AI virtual cell models. In 2025 the Arc Institute released its State model and launched the Virtual Cell Challenge, a public contest to predict unseen responses.`,facts:[`The Mycoplasma simulation tracked 28 modules and 525 genes (2012).`,`Geneformer was pre-trained on about 30 million single cells (2023).`]},"tech:leo":{intro:`A low Earth orbit broadband constellation is a fleet of thousands of small satellites, a few hundred kilometres up, that together cover the whole planet with internet service. Being close to the ground means a signal's round trip takes tens of milliseconds instead of half a second, so video calls and games work, but each satellite is overhead for only a few minutes, so thousands are needed.`,how:`Each satellite sweeps across the sky in about ten minutes, so a user terminal with an electronically steered flat antenna tracks one, then hands over to the next without moving. Signals go up to the satellite and back down to a ground station connected to the internet, or across the constellation by laser. Satellites in a dense shell must dodge each other and other spacecraft, and they are deliberately placed low so that air drag pulls dead ones down within a few years.`,history:`In 1990 Motorola announced Iridium, 66 satellites for voice calls, which went bankrupt in 1999 within a year of service. In November 2016 SpaceX applied to the US regulator for a constellation of 4,425 satellites. In May 2019 SpaceX launched the first 60 Starlink satellites on one Falcon 9 rocket. In October 2020 Starlink opened a public beta in the northern United States at 99 dollars a month. In 2022 Starlink terminals were sent to Ukraine and used by its forces and civilians during the war. In September 2024 Starlink passed 4 million customers, and in April 2025 Amazon launched its first 27 Project Kuiper satellites.`,facts:[`Starlink's first operational launch carried 60 satellites at once (2019).`,`Starlink's satellites orbit at about 550 km, where a round trip takes about 30 ms (2019).`,`SpaceX had launched over 10,000 Starlink satellites by October 2025.`]},"tech:satlaser":{intro:`Laser inter-satellite links let satellites pass data straight to each other through space with beams of infrared light, instead of relaying everything down to a ground station. A constellation becomes a mesh network in the sky, able to serve oceans, deserts and polar regions where no ground station could be built, and to carry long-distance traffic faster than fibre.`,how:`Each satellite carries several optical terminals: a small telescope, a laser and a fine steering mirror. To connect, two satellites moving at nearly 8 kilometres a second must find and hold each other's beam within a tiny fraction of a degree across thousands of kilometres, tracking continuously as they move. Light travels about 47% faster in vacuum than in glass fibre, so over long distances a path through space can beat a cable.`,history:`In November 2001 the European Space Agency's Artemis satellite and the French SPOT-4 made the first laser link between satellites. In 2017 SpaceX said its planned constellation would use laser links. In September 2020 SpaceX launched its first Starlink satellites with laser terminals, for polar coverage. In 2021 every new Starlink satellite was launched with laser links, and in November 2021 SpaceX said its 1.5 generation satellites carried three terminals each. In 2024 SpaceX reported over 9,000 laser terminals in orbit carrying tens of petabytes a day. In 2023 Amazon demonstrated laser links between two Kuiper prototype satellites at 100 gigabits a second.`,facts:[`Starlink's laser mesh carried about 42 petabytes a day (2024).`,`Each Starlink laser link ran at up to 100 gigabits a second (2024).`,`Artemis and SPOT-4 made the first satellite-to-satellite laser link in 2001.`]},"tech:diloco":{intro:`Low-communication training lets a model be trained at several sites that are far apart, without needing a fast network between them. Ordinary distributed training swaps gradients after every batch, hundreds of times a minute, which only works inside one data centre. In this scheme each site trains on its own for hundreds of steps and the sites compare notes only occasionally.`,how:`Every site keeps a full copy of the model and trains it with ordinary methods for, say, 500 steps on its own share of the data. Then each site sends how far its copy has drifted from the shared starting point, and an outer optimiser averages these drifts, with momentum, into a new shared model that everyone adopts. Because the exchange happens rarely, a slow link between sites is enough, and the final quality is close to training everything together.`,history:`In 2016 Jianmin Chen and colleagues at Google compared asynchronous and synchronous training of large models. In November 2023 Arthur Douillard and colleagues at Google DeepMind published DiLoCo, which communicated 500 times less often with nearly the same results. In November 2024 the start-up Prime Intellect finished training INTELLECT-1, a 10-billion-parameter model, on hardware spread across five countries on three continents. In 2024 Nous Research's DisTrO reported cutting communication by even more. In 2025 Prime Intellect trained INTELLECT-2, a 32-billion-parameter reasoning model, on donated GPUs around the world.`,facts:[`DiLoCo exchanged updates 500 times less often than standard training (2023).`,`INTELLECT-1 trained across 5 countries and 3 continents (2024).`,`INTELLECT-1 used about 83% of its compute productively despite the distance (2024).`]},"tech:multidc":{intro:`Multi-datacentre training runs one training job across campuses hundreds of miles apart, so that a model can use more chips than any single site can power and cool. A large AI campus may be limited to a gigawatt or so by its electricity supply; joining several sites with dedicated fibre lets a company treat them as one very large computer.`,how:`Within a site, accelerators are joined by extremely fast local networks. Between sites, dedicated long-distance fibre carries far less traffic per second, with a delay of several milliseconds for every few hundred miles. Training is therefore split so that the chatty parts, such as the layers of one model copy, stay inside a site, while the parts that need only occasional exchange, such as different batches of data, span sites. Low-communication methods make the gap easier to bridge.`,history:`In December 2023 Google said it had trained Gemini Ultra across multiple data centres using its own network. In 2024 reports described Microsoft and OpenAI planning training runs spanning several US sites. In September 2025 Microsoft announced its second Fairwater site in Atlanta, Georgia, linked to its Wisconsin campus about 700 miles away by a dedicated AI wide area network. In November 2025 Microsoft said it had laid about 120,000 miles of dedicated fibre for that network, increasing its total fibre by more than a quarter in a year. In 2025 Microsoft described the result as an “AI superfactory”, with the two campuses training models together.`,facts:[`Microsoft's AI network used about 120,000 miles of dedicated fibre (2025).`,`Its Atlanta and Wisconsin campuses lay about 700 miles apart (2025).`,`Each Fairwater rack was designed for about 140 kilowatts (2025).`]},"tech:smr":{intro:`A small modular reactor is a nuclear power plant of a few hundred megawatts or less, built in a factory in standard units and delivered to the site, rather than constructed as a one-off giant. The appeal is cost and schedule: repeated factory production should be cheaper and faster than bespoke building, and a data centre campus could sit beside its own reactors for steady, carbon-free power.`,how:`Most designs are small enough to cool themselves by natural circulation if power fails, so they need fewer active safety systems, and some are buried below ground. Several units can be added as demand grows. Designs vary widely: some are shrunken versions of ordinary water-cooled reactors, others use molten salt, helium gas or liquid metal as coolant, which allows higher temperatures. All must pass years of regulatory review before any concrete is poured.`,history:`In the 1950s the US Army built small transportable reactors for remote bases, and nuclear submarines have used compact reactors since 1955. In May 2020 Russia's floating plant Akademik Lomonosov, with two small reactors, began commercial operation in Chukotka. In December 2023 China connected the Shidaowan HTR-PM, a pebble-bed gas-cooled plant, to the grid. In November 2023 the American company NuScale cancelled its first planned project in Idaho after costs rose and subscribers withdrew. In October 2024 Google agreed to buy power from Kairos Power's reactors, Amazon invested in X-energy, and Microsoft hired a nuclear team; in 2024 Meta and Oklo also signed agreements. Forecasters expected the first reactors for data centres around 2030.`,facts:[`NuScale's module was designed for 77 megawatts of electricity (2023).`,`In 2024 Google's Kairos agreement covered up to 500 megawatts of power.`]},"tech:orbitaldc":{intro:`An orbital data centre would put computing hardware in space, where sunlight is constant and far stronger than on the ground and there is no need for land, water or a grid connection. The difficulties are heat and launch cost: in vacuum there is no air to carry warmth away, so every watt must be radiated out as infrared light by large panels, and every kilogram must be flown up.`,how:`Solar panels in orbit collect several times more energy per square metre over a day than panels on the ground, with no night in the right orbit. Servers would be cooled by pumping fluid to radiator panels that glow their heat into space. Data would travel to and from the ground by laser or radio, so only results need come down. Radiation damages electronics, nothing can be repaired by hand, and a dense constellation must manage collision risk.`,history:`In 1968 Peter Glaser proposed collecting solar power in orbit and beaming it to Earth. In 2024 the European Commission's ASCEND study concluded that orbital data centres could in principle be environmentally worthwhile. In November 2025 the start-up Starcloud launched Starcloud-1, carrying an NVIDIA H100, and reported training a small model in orbit. In November 2025 Google described Project Suncatcher, a research plan for solar-powered TPU satellites, with test launches planned. In January 2026 SpaceX applied to the US regulator for up to a million satellites to serve as orbital data centres. Forecasters expected serious orbital computing around 2030.`,facts:[`In orbit, solar panels can collect up to about 8 times more energy a year (2025).`,`SpaceX's filing proposed shells between 500 and 2,000 km up (2026).`]},"tech:fusion":{intro:`Fusion power would generate electricity by forcing light atomic nuclei together, as the Sun does, rather than splitting heavy ones. Its fuels are abundant, it leaves no long-lived waste and it cannot melt down. The difficulty is holding a gas hotter than the Sun's core, a plasma, long enough and densely enough that the energy released exceeds the enormous energy poured in.`,how:`In a tokamak, powerful magnets twist the plasma into a doughnut shape so it never touches the walls, and it is heated to over 100 million degrees. High-temperature superconducting tape, which works in stronger fields than older superconductors, allows smaller, cheaper machines. Other designs crush fuel pellets with lasers, or squeeze plasma with pulsed magnetic fields. Even after a net energy gain, turning that heat into electricity reliably and cheaply is a further step.`,history:`In 1997 Europe's JET tokamak in Oxfordshire produced 16 megawatts of fusion power while absorbing about 24. In 2010 construction began in France on ITER, an international tokamak meant to show large net gain. In December 2022 the National Ignition Facility in California got more energy out of a laser-crushed fuel pellet than the lasers delivered to it, the first such gain. In September 2021 Commonwealth Fusion Systems tested a 20-tesla high-temperature superconducting magnet. In May 2023 Microsoft agreed to buy electricity from Helion Energy later in the decade, the first such power deal. In 2025 Commonwealth began assembling its SPARC tokamak in Devens, Massachusetts. Forecasters expected fusion plants to supply data centres around 2034.`,facts:[`The National Ignition Facility got 3.15 megajoules out for 2.05 in (2022).`,`JET set a record of 69 megajoules of fusion energy in one pulse in 2023.`]},"tech:qubits":{intro:`A superconducting qubit is a tiny electrical circuit, printed on a chip and chilled to a few thousandths of a degree above absolute zero, that behaves like a single quantum object. It can hold a mixture of 0 and 1 at once, and two qubits can be entangled so that their states are linked. Such chips became the most common basis for building quantum computers.`,how:`The circuit is a loop of superconducting metal containing a Josephson junction, a thin insulating barrier, which makes its energy levels uneven so that only the lowest two are used as 0 and 1. Microwave pulses down coaxial cables perform operations and read the result. Everything sits inside a dilution refrigerator, a chandelier of gold-plated stages cooled to about 15 millikelvin, because the slightest heat or stray field destroys the delicate state within microseconds.`,history:`In 1999 Yasunobu Nakamura and colleagues at NEC in Japan demonstrated the first superconducting qubit. In 2007 Robert Schoelkopf's group at Yale introduced the transmon, a design far less sensitive to electrical noise, now the standard. In May 2016 IBM put a 5-qubit processor online for anyone to use. In October 2019 Google reported in Nature that its 53-qubit Sycamore chip had performed in about 200 seconds a sampling task it argued would take a supercomputer far longer, a claim IBM disputed. In November 2022 IBM unveiled Osprey with 433 qubits, and in December 2023 Condor with 1,121.`,facts:[`Superconducting qubits run at about 15 millikelvin, colder than deep space (2016).`,`The first superconducting qubit held its state for about 2 nanoseconds (1999).`,`Google's Sycamore had 53 working qubits (2019).`]},"tech:qec":{intro:`Quantum error correction protects fragile quantum information by spreading one reliable “logical” qubit across many noisy physical ones. Qubits lose their state within microseconds and every operation on them is slightly wrong, so useful computation needs errors caught and undone as they happen. The crucial test is whether adding more physical qubits makes the logical one better rather than worse.`,how:`In a surface code, a patch of qubits on a grid holds one logical qubit, and extra “measurement” qubits between them are read repeatedly to detect disturbances without ever measuring the stored information itself, which would destroy it. A classical computer decodes this stream of warnings and works out which corrections to apply. Below a certain physical error rate, called the threshold, enlarging the patch makes the logical error rate fall, and above it, grow.`,history:`In 1995 Peter Shor at AT&T showed that quantum error correction was possible at all, and in 1996 Andrew Steane published another code. In 1997 Alexei Kitaev proposed the surface code, which needs only connections between neighbouring qubits. In February 2023 Google reported in Nature that a 49-qubit patch slightly outperformed a 17-qubit one, the first sign of going the right way. In December 2024 Google announced Willow, a 105-qubit chip, and reported in Nature that each time the patch grew, the logical error rate halved: below the threshold at last. In December 2023 Harvard and QuEra reported logical qubits in a different technology, arrays of neutral atoms held by lasers.`,facts:[`Willow's logical error rate halved with each step up in patch size (2024).`,`Its best logical qubit was about 2.4 times better than its physical ones (2024).`]},"tech:logical":{intro:`Logical qubits at scale means a machine with hundreds of error-corrected qubits, enough to run real quantum algorithms rather than demonstrations. With a few hundred good logical qubits, chemists could simulate molecules and materials, such as catalysts and battery electrolytes, that are beyond any ordinary supercomputer because their electrons' quantum behaviour grows impossibly complex to calculate.`,how:`Each logical qubit needs many physical ones, perhaps dozens to a thousand depending on the code and the hardware's quality, so hundreds of logical qubits mean tens or hundreds of thousands of physical ones. That demands chips joined into modules, refrigerators larger than any built before, and tens of thousands of microwave control lines. Error decoding must also keep up in real time, since corrections must be chosen faster than new errors appear.`,history:`In 2022 IBM published a roadmap promising error-corrected machines by the end of the decade. In 2023 IBM and others proposed “quantum low-density parity check” codes, which need far fewer physical qubits per logical one than the surface code. In June 2025 IBM detailed Starling, a machine it planned to finish by the end of the decade with 200 logical qubits able to run 100 million operations. In 2025 Google's roadmap also aimed at a long-lived logical qubit as its next milestone. Forecasters expected hundreds of logical qubits around 2029.`,facts:[`In 2025 IBM said Starling would hold 200 logical qubits and run 100 million operations.`,`IBM said it would need roughly 10 times fewer physical qubits with the new codes (2023).`]},"tech:ftqc":{intro:`A fault-tolerant quantum computer would run billions of operations without an uncorrected error, enough for the algorithms that make quantum computing famous. It would simulate chemistry exactly, speed up some optimisation problems, and, less happily, break the public-key cryptography that protects most internet traffic and banking, which is why governments have begun replacing those codes.`,how:`Fault tolerance means every part of the machine, including the error correction itself, keeps working when its components are imperfect. The hardest operations to protect are those outside the easily corrected set, which need a costly process called magic state distillation. Running Shor's factoring algorithm on a 2,048-bit key is estimated to need thousands of logical qubits and perhaps a million physical ones, with error decoders running continuously alongside.`,history:`In 1994 Peter Shor at Bell Labs published an algorithm that factors large numbers quickly on a quantum computer, breaking RSA encryption in principle. In 1996 Lov Grover published a quantum search algorithm. In 2019 the US National Academies judged that breaking RSA-2048 would need a machine far beyond any then planned. In August 2024 the US standards body NIST published its first post-quantum encryption standards, for codes a quantum computer could not break. In June 2025 IBM named a machine planned after Starling, Blue Jay, with 2,000 logical qubits and a billion operations. Forecasters expected fault-tolerant machines around 2032.`,facts:[`In 2025 IBM set Blue Jay's target at 2,000 logical qubits and a billion operations.`,`NIST published its first post-quantum cryptography standards in August 2024.`]}};export{e as PEDIA_MODERN};